{"abstract":"We investigate the use of Facebook in the Danish system over a two-month period in 2021, with an increased political polarization, during the COVID-19 lockdown. Based on the analysis of 5093 posts, we find indications of how party-system dynamics shape niche parties and catch-all parties’ use of Facebook. While catch-all party members share political information with relatively low reach, niche parties are much more inclined to criticize opponents, a strategy that often produces higher interactivity. Finally, we discuss the impact of party-system dynamics on the strategic use of Facebook among different parties. Our findings indicate that the social media use of niche parties with irrelevant features is often shaped by a centrifugal logic (competition towards the poles), while the social media use of catch-all parties is often shaped by a centripetal dynamic (competition towards the center).","author_count":4,"contributors":["Aagaard, Peter","Schwartz, Sander Andreas","Nygaard, Line","Larsen, Malene Teresa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-811900"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Catch all parties","Equalization","Facebook","Moderate pluralism","Niche parties","Normalization","Party system","Polarization","Populism","Social media"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"how party system dynamics shape political parties use of facebook between elections","src":["ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-RUC"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/journalmedia6010006","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-811900","identifier":[{"id":"105001231506","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ruc-8ff245e0-a1d0-4dfd-88e3-04ee7e2df7d7"],"keyword":["Catch-all parties","Equalization","Facebook","Moderate pluralism","Niche parties","Normalization","Party system","Polarization","Populism","Social media"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A471A92F08DC870416E1C9E0AA1937A5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/journalmedia6010006","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://rucforsk.ruc.dk/ws/files/110056443/journalmedia-06-00006-v2.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ruc roskilde university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-RUC","l3nl":"Roskilde University","l3nn":"RUC Roskilde University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"RUC Roskilde University","namevar":["Roskilde University, Department of Communication and Arts, Centre for Digital Citizenship","Roskilde University, Department of Social Sciences and Business","Roskilde University, Department of Social Sciences and Business, Roskilde School of Governance","Roskilde University, Department of Social Sciences and Business, Transformation and Ordering of Political Institutions"],"nora_code":"DI-U-RUC","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Peter","key":"aagaardpau","last":"Aagaard","locid":"1981042e-08ab-4e4b-8c6f-ef1480d2a6e4","name":"Aagaard, Peter","orcid":"0000-0003-3588-9156","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Sander Andreas","key":"schwartzpau","last":"Schwartz","locid":"88ff7846-6ae7-4f68-9807-db2963320789","name":"Schwartz, Sander Andreas","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Line","key":"nygaardpau","last":"Nygaard","name":"Nygaard, Line","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Malene Teresa","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Malene Teresa","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/journalmedia6010006","issn":["26735172"],"issue":"1","journal":"Journalism and Media","paperid":"6","status":"p","title":"Journalism and Media","volume":"6"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105001231506"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Roskilde University","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ruc-8ff245e0-a1d0-4dfd-88e3-04ee7e2df7d7"],"src":["RUC Roskilde University"],"title":"How Party-System Dynamics Shape Political Parties’ Use of Facebook Between Elections","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-811900","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Background: Surgery and anesthesia in neonates and infants are performed only when necessary due to their immature physiological systems and limited cardiorespiratory reserves, rendering them particularly susceptible to complications. Effective pain management in the context of major surgical procedures presents a considerable challenge. Untreated pain activates cellular and humoral pathways that are detrimental to recovery. Although opioids are effective analgesics, their use is associated with hypoventilation and may exacerbate the physiological apnea commonly observed in newborns. This can extend the requirement for positive pressure ventilation, increasing the risks of both immediate and long-term adverse outcomes. Epidural anesthesia and analgesia offer a promising alternative by providing effective pain relief while reducing opioid consumption, thus minimizing respiratory impairment and promoting more rapid recovery. This scoping review aims to map existing knowledge, to shed light on knowledge gaps, and lay the groundwork for further research. Methods: This review will follow the JBI methodology and adhere to the PRISMA guidelines for Scoping Reviews. A comprehensive search will be conducted across peer-reviewed databases, published literature, online resources, ongoing studies, and gray literature to identify all available evidence on epidural anesthesia and analgesia in thoracic and abdominal surgeries in neonates and infants. Results: The findings from the included studies will be synthesized narratively and presented with tables and figures to ensure a clear and structured overview. Conclusion: This scoping review aims to summarize the current evidence on epidural anesthesia and analgesia in neonates and infants. It will highlight well-researched areas, identify gaps in the literature, and outline existing knowledge and unmet research needs in this field.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Larsen, Line Gry","Hansen, Tom Giedsing"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-812664"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Larsen, Line Gry"],"keyword":["Epidural","Infant","Laparotomy","Neonate","Scoping","Thoracotomy"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"epidural anesthesia and analgesia in neonates and infants protocol for a scoping review","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/aas.70029","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-812664","identifier":[{"id":"PMC12009654","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"40254031","type":"pmid"},{"id":"105003275545","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-b1492eaa-61e4-4de8-a7c6-92c23fe40be3"],"keyword":["epidural","infant","laparotomy","neonate","scoping","thoracotomy"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-BA15B88EAE362DB0A30139422164D985"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/aas.70029","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/289342090/Acta_Anaesthesiol_Scand_-_2025_-_Larsen_-_Epidural_anesthesia_and_analgesia_in_neonates_and_infants_Protocol_for_a_scoping.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of Anaesthesiology and Intensive Care (Odense)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Line Gry","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"9e779ea0-e653-4cd5-94e0-a583ca1eb0e6","name":"Larsen, Line Gry","orcid":"0000-0002-0683-4503","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Tom Geidsing","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"5f66539d-cd24-4c21-8e6b-fefbcdcb0032","name":"Hansen, Tom Giedsing","name_org":"Hansen, Tom Geidsing","orcid":"0000-0002-5896-9768","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40254031"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/aas.70029","issn":["00015172"],"issue":"5","journal":"Acta Anaesthesiologica Scandinavica","paperid":"e70029","publisher":"Wiley","status":"p","title":"Acta Anaesthesiologica Scandinavica","volume":"69"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105003275545"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-b1492eaa-61e4-4de8-a7c6-92c23fe40be3"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Epidural anesthesia and analgesia in neonates and infants : Protocol for a scoping review","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-812664","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Background: Schizophrenia and bipolar disorder are characterized by social cognitive impairments, and recent research has identified alterations of the social brain. However, it is unknown whether familial high risk (FHR) of these disorders is associated with neurobiological alterations already present in childhood. Methods: As part of the Danish High Risk and Resilience Study–VIA 11, we examined children at FHR of schizophrenia (n = 121, 50% female) or bipolar disorder (n = 75, 47% female) and population-based control children (PBCs) (n = 128, 48% female). Using functional magnetic resonance imaging and dynamic causal modeling, we investigated brain activation and effective connectivity during the social cognition paradigm from the Human Connectome Project. Results: We found similar activation of the mentalizing network across groups, including visual area V5, the dorsomedial prefrontal cortex, and the posterior superior temporal sulcus (pSTS). Nonetheless, both FHR groups showed aberrant brain connectivity in the form of increased feedforward connectivity from left V5 to pSTS compared with PBCs. Children at FHR of schizophrenia had reduced intrinsic connectivity in bilateral V5 compared with PBCs, whereas children at FHR of bipolar disorder showed increased reciprocal connectivity between the left dorsomedial prefrontal cortex and the pSTS, increased intrinsic connectivity in the right pSTS, and reduced feedforward connectivity from the right pSTS to the dorsomedial prefrontal cortex compared with PBCs. Conclusions: Our results provide first-time evidence of aberrant brain connectivity in the mentalizing network of children at FHR of schizophrenia or FHR of bipolar disorder. Longitudinal research is warranted to clarify whether aberrant brain connectivity during mentalizing constitutes an endophenotype associated with the development of a mental disorder later in life.","author_count":23,"contributors":["Veddum, Lotte","Bliksted, Vibeke Fuglsang","Zhou, Yuan","Andreassen, Anna Krogh","Knudsen, Christina Bruun","Greve, Aja Neergaard","Steffensen, Nanna Lawaetz","Birk, Merete","Hemager, Nicoline","Brandt, Julie Marie","Gregersen, Maja","Johnsen, Line Korsgaard","Larsen, Kit Melissa","Baaré, William Frans Christiaan","Madsen, Kathrine Skak","Siebner, Hartwig Roman","Plessen, Kerstin Jessica","Thorup, Anne Amalie Elgaard","Østergaard, Leif","Nordentoft, Merete","Mors, Ole","Lund, Torben Ellegaard","Dietz, Martin"],"cuid":["S02-087.50-785221"],"filters":{"added":"2024-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Asia, East","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Dietz, Martin","Veddum, Lotte"],"country":["China","Switzerland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","CAS - Institute of Psychology","Lausanne University Hospital","University of Chinese Academy of Sciences"],"keyword":["Bipolar disorder","Brain connectivity","Familial high risk","Functional magnetic resonance imaging","Neurodevelopmental disorders","Schizophrenia","Severe mental illness","Social brain","fMRI"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-NP","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR"],"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-LUND"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-NP-LUND","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"brain activation and aberrant effective connectivity in the mentalizing network of preadolescent children at familial high risk of schizophrenia or bipolar disorder","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bpsc.2024.08.004","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-087.50-785221","identifier":[{"id":"39182726","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85207329618","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-dd315dcd-2d3a-4