,"first":"Camilla Ikast","key":"ottosencamillaikastpau","last":"Ottosen","name":"Ottosen, Camilla Ikast","rank":26,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten","key":"sengeløvmortenpau","last":"Sengeløv","name":"Sengeløv, Morten","rank":27,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Kristoffer Grundtvig","key":"skaarupkristoffergrundtvigpau","last":"Skaarup","name":"Skaarup, Kristoffer Grundtvig","rank":28,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"9 10","corresponding":null,"first":"Scott D","key":"solomonscottdpau","last":"Solomon","name":"Solomon, Scott D","rank":29,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"11","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin J","key":"landraymartinjpau","last":"Landray","name":"Landray, Martin J","rank":30,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Gunnar H","key":"gislasongunnarhpau","last":"Gislason","name":"Gislason, Gunnar H","rank":31,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lars","key":"køberlarspau","last":"Køber","name":"Køber, Lars","rank":32,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Line","key":"ralfkiaerlinepau","last":"Ralfkiaer","name":"Ralfkiaer, Line","rank":33,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Joshua","key":"nealonjoshuapau","last":"Nealon","name":"Nealon, Joshua","rank":34,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Pradeesh","key":"sivapalanpradeeshpau","last":"Sivapalan","name":"Sivapalan, Pradeesh","rank":35,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"12","corresponding":null,"first":"Cyril Jean-Marie","key":"martelcyriljeanmariepau","last":"Martel","name":"Martel, Cyril Jean-Marie","rank":36,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens Ulrik Stæhr","key":"jensenjensulrikstæhrpau","last":"Jensen","name":"Jensen, Jens Ulrik Stæhr","rank":37,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Tor","key":"bieringsørensentorpau","last":"Biering-Sørensen","name":"Biering-Sørensen, Tor","rank":38,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40888720"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1056/nejmoa2509907","issn":["00284793","15334406"],"issn_org":["00284793"],"journal":"The New England Journal of Medicine","publisher":"Massachussetts Medical Society","status":"e","title":"The New England Journal of Medicine"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Hospitals","Region Zealand","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["E-Published (ahead of print)"]},"sid":["aau-4a14a2a7-1f37-4402-985f-7140e5d6b8e6"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"High-Dose Influenza Vaccine Effectiveness against Hospitalization in Older Adults","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-823439","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Introduction Childhood obesity is a growing global public health issue, with significant implications for children's physical and psychological well-being. Schools offer a structured environment for implementing health-promoting interventions to address this challenge. This study examines how organizational conditions within schools influence frontline workers' (e.g., teachers and pedagogic staff) early implementation behavior in a school-based health intervention. Insights aim to inform strategies for enhancing the sustainability and effectiveness of such programs.Methods The study utilizes a qualitative design, conducting 11 focus group interviews across 11 of the 12 intervention schools participating in the Generation Healthy Kids (GHK) intervention program in Denmark. Using the Consolidated Framework for Implementation Research (CFIR) and the COM-B Model for Behavior Change, the interviews explored organizational factors shaping frontline workers' capabilities, opportunities, and motivation for implementing GHK components. Thematic analysis, guided by predefined theoretical constructs, was applied to identify key patterns in the data.Results The findings highlight organizational conditions within schools as critical to frontline workers' early implementation behavior. Capability was linked to training and resources, though time constraints hindered effective application. Opportunity was shaped by structural factors such as limited access to facilities, causing logistical challenges. Motivation was influenced by alignment with existing workflows, reflective beliefs about the program's relevance, and tension for change due to perceived pupil needs. Leadership emerged as a cross-cutting factor, with engaged leaders enhancing implementation through active support and resource coordination.Discussion The study underscores the interplay between organizational conditions and frontline workers' behaviors in implementing health programs. Addressing logistical barriers, fostering supportive leadership, and ensuring alignment with school priorities are essential for successful implementation. These findings highlight the need for adaptive, school-specific implementation strategies to sustain health-promoting interventions like GHK and suggest stakeholder engagement to further optimize implementation processes.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Nielsen, Jonas Vestergaard","Koch, Sofie","Henriksen, Tine Hyldahl","Krolner, Rikke Fredenslund","Lund, Line","Brautsch, Louise Ayoe Sparvath","Ulrikkeholm, Line Kattai","Damsgaard, Camilla Trab","Larsen, Malte Nejst","Skovgaard, Thomas"],"cuid":["S02-097.62-814046"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Koch, Sofie"],"keyword":["COM B","Consolidated Framework for Implementation Research","Frontline workers","Implementation research","Qualitative","School based program"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"understanding school frontline workers early implementation behavior insights from the generation healthy kids study in denmark","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fspor.2025.1534123","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["The author(s) declare that financial support was received for the research and/or publication of this article. The research is funded by the Novo Nordisk Foundation (grant no. NNF22SA0077224). The funder was not involved in the study design, collection, analysis, interpretation of data, the writing of this article, or the decision to submit it for publication."],"id":"S02-097.62-814046","identifier":[{"id":"001508760200001","type":"wos"},{"id":"40529408","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC12171354","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"105008098105","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-6fe3caa2-8d49-44d3-aaf3-63d744acec9d","sdu-85835e5c-af7e-4dfd-8471-b0663ede8eb6"],"keyword":["Com-b","Consolidated framework for implementation research","Frontline workers","Implementation research","Qualitative","School-based program"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-DC4A1650B30C6F206B32663C9DF7D149"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fspor.2025.1534123","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/291731035/fspor-2-1534123.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/454801578/fspor-2-1534123.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Sports Science and Clinical Biomechanics","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Sports Science and Clinical Biomechanics, Research Unit of Active Living"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Nutrition, Exercise and Sports, Nutrition and Health"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonas Vestergaard","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"1e9161ad-ac9e-4797-827a-cd8bedb14a8d","name":"Nielsen, Jonas Vestergaard","orcid":"0000-0001-8800-5073","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sofie","key":"kochpau","last":"Koch","locid":"48dee074-f7b2-4dd0-a933-3fde0261d5f8","name":"Koch, Sofie","orcid":"0000-0002-9404-5001","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Tine Hyldahl","key":"henriksenpau","last":"Henriksen","locid":"a9177d62-4d53-4847-b4b2-f5e6f2a314fa","name":"Henriksen, Tine Hyldahl","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rikke Fredenslund","key":"krølnerpau","last":"Krølner","locid":"7c22d49c-cc2e-49f8-a06a-ae4e659224cd","name":"Krølner, Rikke","name_org":"Krølner, Rikke Fredenslund","orcid":"0000-0002-4928-4310","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Line","key":"lundpau","last":"Lund","locid":"90964105-298e-439e-8380-258ac418a7cb","name":"Lund, Line","orcid":"0000-0001-5338-9644","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Louise Ayoe Sparvath","key":"brautschpau","last":"Brautsch","locid":"3e9d5f7b-8b64-46af-9944-9e80cd234ac2","name":"Brautsch, Louise Ayoe Sparvath","orcid":"0009-0001-2954-1172","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Line Kattai","key":"ulrikkeholmpau","last":"Ulrikkeholm","locid":"e5deccbb-430e-47ef-ad87-974e686ec5a1","name":"Ulrikkeholm, Line Kattai","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Camilla Trab","key":"damsgaardpau","last":"Damsgaard","locid":"5ea9cc09-17f1-46a3-9e4e-fa5490abd815","name":"Damsgaard, Camilla Trab","orcid":"0000-0002-5013-0562","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Malte Nejst","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"3ab5dd9c-52d8-4dfb-adb0-62db96cf76a2","name":"Larsen, Malte Nejst","orcid":"0000-0002-2600-7126","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Thomas","key":"skovgaardpau","last":"Skovgaard","locid":"2c6eebd4-6fdf-4819-bf31-6ae3c7e0b582","name":"Skovgaard, Thomas","orcid":"0000-0001-6729-4988","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40529408"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3389/fspor.2025.1534123","issn":["26249367"],"journal":"Frontiers in Sports and Active Living","paperid":"1534123","publisher":"Frontiers Media","status":"p","title":"Frontiers in Sports and Active Living","volume":"7"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105008098105"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-6fe3caa2-8d49-44d3-aaf3-63d744acec9d","sdu-85835e5c-af7e-4dfd-8471-b0663ede8eb6"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Understanding school frontline workers' early implementation behavior : insights from the generation healthy kids study in Denmark","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-097.62-814046","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Context Thyroid hormones are critical for neural development, and during the first trimester of pregnancy, the fetus relies fully on maternal thyroid hormone production. Objective To investigate the associations between maternal thyroid hormone levels in the first trimester with the child's school performance, risk of attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD), and autism spectrum disorder (ASD). Methods Information from the Copenhagen Primary Care Laboratory Pregnancy Database on first trimester TSH and free thyroxine measurements in mothers of children born in 2000 through 2014 were linked with information on the child's standardized test scores in school, ADHD (patient record diagnoses and medication), and ASD (patient record diagnoses) until the end of 2018. Associations of TSH and free thyroxine with the outcomes were individually assessed by linear mixed models and Cox regression models. The analyses were stratified by preexisting maternal thyroid disorders. Results TSH measurements were available for 17 909 mother-child dyads. Among those with children born in 2000 through 2009, 6126 had a standardized school test score and were analyzed for the association between maternal thyroid hormone levels and child's school performance, and no support for an association was found. The association between thyroid hormone levels and child's risk of ADHD and ASD were analyzed for the 17 909 dyads and with no support for an association between thyroid hormone levels and these neurodevelopmental disorders. Stratification by preexisting maternal thyroid disorders did not affect the results. Conclusion We found no evidence for associations between first trimester maternal thyroid hormone levels and child's school performance, or risk of ADHD or ASD.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Møllehave, Line Tang","Grand, Mia Klinten","Kriegbaum, Margit","Andersen, Christen Lykkegaard","Lind, Bent Struer","van Vliet, Nicolien Alien","van Heemst, Diana","Strandberg-Larsen, Katrine"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-800475"],"filters":{"added":"2025-04","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Netherlands"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Leiden University"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"maternal thyroid function in early pregnancy and offspring school performance and neurodevelopmental disorders","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-800475","identifier":[{"id":"38781538","type":"pmid"},{"id":"105000441150","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-c42184aa-dac3-4768-a76d-a6ef69500740"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-063A81BA391D10CC30EF5F05FE7BB181"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Bispebjerg and Frederiksberg"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, External Centres, Forskningsenheden for Almen Praksis","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Public Health, Section of General Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"nl","key":"leiden university","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Leiden University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Line Tang","key":"møllehavepau","last":"Møllehave","name":"Møllehave, Line Tang","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Mia Klinten","key":"grandpau","last":"Grand","locid":"43f57b9c-d90a-4d31-96d9-3a3348bdb369","name":"Grand, Mia Klinten","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Margit","key":"kriegbaumpau","last":"Kriegbaum","locid":"c789838e-6fa6-4924-829d-ebc0fd79964e","name":"Kriegbaum, Margit","orcid":"0000-0001-9078-1194","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","first":"Christen Lykkegaard","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"2df222fc-0731-4cf1-a68b-650248385782","name":"Andersen, Christen Lykkegaard","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Bent Struer","key":"lindpau","last":"Lind","name":"Lind, Bent Struer","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Nicolien Alien","key":"vanvlietpau","last":"van Vliet","name":"van Vliet, Nicolien Alien","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Diana","key":"vanheemstpau","last":"van Heemst","name":"van Heemst, Diana","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Katrine","key":"strandberglarsenpau","last":"Strandberg-Larsen","locid":"5a443538-3866-40a3-b93e-b4d4be24a83a","name":"Strandberg-Larsen, Katrine","orcid":"0000-0001-7061-3767","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38781538"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1210/clinem/dgae358","issn":["0021972X","19457197"],"issn_org":["0021972X"],"issue":"4","journal":"Journal of Clinical Endocrinology and Metabolism","status":"p","title":"Journal of Clinical Endocrinology and Metabolism","title_org":"The Journal of clinical endocrinology and metabolism","volume":"110"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105000441150"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-c42184aa-dac3-4768-a76d-a6ef69500740"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Maternal thyroid function in early pregnancy and offspring school performance and neurodevelopmental disorders","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-800475","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Forensic odontology identification largely depends on comparing dental work like fillings and crowns with the dental records of potential victims. This process can be challenging, especially regarding victims with minimal or no dental work. Alternatively, 3D tooth morphology can be used for identification by automated dental surface similarity scoring. However, high-resolution 3D intraoral photo scans contain hundreds of thousands of datapoints from each individual jaw, making database searches difficult and slow. Here, we reduce full 3D scans to keypoints, which are small points located in areas of high curvature on tooth surfaces. We use Difference of Curvature (DoC) for robust keypoint detection and evaluate different keypoint representation methods to distinguish between scans of the same individual and scans of different individuals, assigning them a similarity score.The results demonstrate that combining DoC with the Signature of Histograms of OrienTations (SHOT) representation method effectively separates matches from mismatches. This indicates the potential for automatic scoring of dental surface similarity. This can be valuable for forensic odontology identification, especially in cases where traditional methods are limited by the lack of dental work.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Kofod Petersen, Anika","Forgie, Andrew","Villesen, Palle","Staun Larsen, Line"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-807722"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Kofod Petersen, Anika"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Glasgow"],"keyword":["3D dental comparison","Biometric identification","Disaster Victim Identification","Forensic odontology identification","Keypoint detection"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"000000000003d dental similarity quantification in forensic odontology identification","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.forsciint.2025.112462","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-807722","identifier":[{"id":"105001866397","type":"scopus"},{"id":"40186981","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-e5d02f2e-6790-4418-9ae0-87d635280d8f"],"keyword":["3D dental comparison","Biometric identification","Disaster victim identification","Forensic odontology identification","Keypoint description","Keypoint detection"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-71DCD39253595560F5E2E880F0232C43"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.forsciint.2025.112462","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Forensic Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of glasgow","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Glasgow","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anika","key":"kofodpetersenpau","last":"Kofod Petersen","locid":"66df1431-908c-44b2-a391-2215ee5b7377","name":"Kofod Petersen, Anika","orcid":"0000-0001-5913-8723","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andrew","key":"forgiepau","last":"Forgie","name":"Forgie, Andrew","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Palle","key":"villesenpau","last":"Villesen","locid":"c1c4bd28-befd-4150-bbbb-10138e528d53","name":"Villesen, Palle","orcid":"0000-0001-7589-8781","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Line","key":"staunlarsenpau","last":"Staun Larsen","locid":"35e2ae60-25db-4a15-893f-dc8906e2bccf","name":"Staun Larsen, Line","orcid":"0000-0002-4155-5884","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40186981"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.forsciint.2025.112462","issn":["03790738","18726283"],"issn_org":["03790738"],"journal":"Forensic Science International","paperid":"112462","publisher":"Elsevier Ireland Ltd","status":"p","title":"Forensic Science International","volume":"370"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105001866397"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-e5d02f2e-6790-4418-9ae0-87d635280d8f"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"3D dental similarity quantification in forensic odontology identification","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-807722","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Purpose: Genetics of Common Variable Immunodeficiency (CVID) is complex and not fully elucidated. This study presents the clinical and genetic findings of a Danish CVID cohort and investigate whether initial genetic findings can be re-classified upon re-evaluation years later in time. Methods: From 2016 to 2021, individuals with CVID or a CVID-like-phenotype were examined using whole exome or whole genome sequencing in combination with comprehensive gene-panels. The results were re-evaluated to ensure up-to-date American College of Medical Genetics and Genomics (ACMG) classification after a median of 3.9 years. Further, a clinical-interpretation-algorithm is proposed. Results: Of 69 enrolled individuals, 57 met the current ESID-CVID-criteria of whom 29 (51%) had a genetic find. In total 67 ACMG class 3 to 5 variants were detected in 39 different genes. Class 3 variants (variants of uncertain significance (VUS)) accounted for 81% in the initial analysis. Upon re-evaluation 17 of 54 (31%) of the originally reported VUS were re-classified to a different ACMG-class or excluded. The developed clinical-interpretation-algorithm demonstrated high interobserver-agreement. A “definite/probable” disease causing (or contributing) genetic variant was found in 19% of the CVID-cohort and a “possible” in 18%. Conclusion: A genetic cause of CVID could be identified in a minority of CVID-individuals, whereas the majority had no or uncertain genetic findings. Re-evaluation of genetic results over time is recommended, though VUS remain a significant challenge in CVID-genetics. Therefore, continued research in both CVID-genetics and in non-genetic causes of CVID is needed.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Drabe, Camilla Heldbjerg","Laustsen, Mira Marie","Marquart, Hanne Vibeke","Hartling, Hans Jakob","Marvig, Rasmus L.","Helweg-Larsen, Jannik","Hansen, Ann-Brit Eg","Lundgren, Jens","Helleberg, Marie","Borgwardt, Line","Katzenstein, Terese L."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-819375"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Antibody deficiency","Common Variable Immunodeficiency","Inborn errors of immunity","Next generation sequencing","Whole exome sequencing","Whole genome sequencing"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"genetics in a danish common variable immunodeficiency cohort","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10875-025-01896-w","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-819375","identifier":[{"id":"PMC12129841","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"105007079648","type":"scopus"},{"id":"40455168","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-73914d78-e7af-4e09-bc26-37dc5eaf1027"],"keyword":["Antibody deficiency","Common variable immunodeficiency","Inborn errors of immunity","Next generation sequencing","Whole exome sequencing","Whole genome sequencing"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-165171F02A0CA35238423A5782ECD3AB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/453519522/s10875-025-01896-w.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10875-025-01896-w","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Clinical immunology","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Camilla Heldbjerg","key":"drabepau","last":"Drabe","name":"Drabe, Camilla Heldbjerg","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Mira Marie","key":"laustsenpau","last":"Laustsen","name":"Laustsen, Mira Marie","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Hanne Vibeke","key":"marquartpau","last":"Marquart","locid":"9b5af5d9-9f83-4dd4-a91c-140cd128fb8c","name":"Marquart, Hanne Vibeke","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Hans Jakob","key":"hartlingpau","last":"Hartling","name":"Hartling, Hans Jakob","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Rasmus L.","key":"marvigpau","last":"Marvig","name":"Marvig, Rasmus L.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jannik","key":"helweglarsenpau","last":"Helweg-Larsen","name":"Helweg-Larsen, Jannik","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","first":"Ann-Brit Eg","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"29b6e1f5-8374-4247-a7bd-76de887cc30d","name":"Hansen, Ann-Brit Eg","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jens","key":"lundgrenpau","last":"Lundgren","name":"Lundgren, Jens","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Marie","key":"hellebergpau","last":"Helleberg","name":"Helleberg, Marie","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Line","key":"borgwardtpau","last":"Borgwardt","name":"Borgwardt, Line","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Terese L.","key":"katzensteinpau","last":"Katzenstein","name":"Katzenstein, Terese L.","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40455168"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s10875-025-01896-w","issn":["02719142","15732592"],"issn_org":["02719142"],"journal":"Journal of Clinical Immunology","paperid":"102","status":"p","title":"Journal of Clinical Immunology","volume":"45"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105007079648"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-73914d78-e7af-4e09-bc26-37dc5eaf1027"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Genetics in a Danish Common Variable Immunodeficiency Cohort","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-819375","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"As populations age and workforces decline, the need for accessible health assessment methods grows. The merging of accessible and affordable sensors such as inertial measurement units (IMUs) and advanced machine learning techniques now enables gait assessment beyond traditional laboratory settings. A total of 52 participants walked at three speeds while carrying a smartphone-sized IMU in natural positions (hand, trouser pocket, or jacket pocket). A previously trained Convolutional Neural Network and Long Short-Term Memory (CNN-LSTM)-based machine learning model predicted gait events, which were then used to calculate stride time, stance time, swing time, and double support time. Stride time predictions were highly accurate (<5% error), while stance and swing times exhibited moderate variability and double support time showed the highest errors (>20%). Despite these variations, moderate-to-strong correlations between the predicted and experimental spatiotemporal gait parameters suggest the feasibility of IMU-based gait tracking in real-world settings. These associations preserved inter-subject patterns that are relevant for detecting gait disorders. Our study demonstrated the feasibility of extracting clinically relevant gait parameters using IMU data mimicking smartphone use, especially parameters with longer durations such as stride time. Robustness across sensor locations and walking speeds supports deep learning on single-IMU data as a viable tool for remote gait monitoring.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Larsen, Aske Gye","Sadolin, Line Ø.","Thomsen, Trine Rolighed","Oliveira, Anderson Souza"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-823587"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Oliveira, Anderson Souza"],"keyword":["Adult","Digital health","Female","Gait Analysis methods","Gait analysis","Gait physiology","Humans","IMU","Machine learning","Male","Neural Networks Computer","Remote monitoring","Smartphone","Walking physiology"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"extraction of clinically relevant temporal gait parameters from imu sensors mimicking the use of smartphones","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/s25144470","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-823587","identifier":[{"id":"105011535555","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC12299727","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"40732598","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["aau-6903ab62-ca01-443d-bad2-48f0b7cd1c05"],"keyword":["Adult","Digital health","Female","Gait Analysis/methods","Gait analysis","Gait/physiology","Humans","IMU","Machine Learning","Male","Neural Networks, Computer","Remote monitoring","Smartphone","Walking/physiology"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-F2674BEFF117AEC92055CBAAFFB47E68"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/s25144470","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/790776504/Open_Access_Article.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, Department of Chemistry and Bioscience","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, Department of Chemistry and Bioscience, Section for Bioscience and Engineering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Aske G","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"46714b8a-5f5a-43f8-b9a9-a59f8255c179","name":"Larsen, Aske Gye","name_org":"Larsen, Aske G","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Line Ø","key":"sadolinpau","last":"Sadolin","locid":"613d453c-10e0-451e-8317-66aeec7bd8ff","name":"Sadolin, Line Ø.","name_org":"Sadolin, Line Ø","orcid":"0009-0006-2187-2125","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Trine R","key":"thomsenpau","last":"Thomsen","locid":"2a598ca5-d398-4f6f-a07a-e27982669ee8","name":"Thomsen, Trine Rolighed","name_org":"Thomsen, Trine R","orcid":"0000-0002-7393-9372","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anderson S","key":"oliveirapau","last":"Oliveira","locid":"52dfbf20-ae09-400b-aabf-b90a8b84f69c","name":"Oliveira, Anderson Souza","name_org":"Oliveira, Anderson S","orcid":"0000-0003-2186-8100","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40732598"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/s25144470","issn":["14248220","14248239"],"issn_org":["14248220"],"issue":"14","journal":"Sensors","paperid":"4470","publisher":"MDPI AG","publisher_org":"M D P I AG","status":"p","title":"Sensors","title_org":"Sensors (Basel, Switzerland)","volume":"25"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105011535555"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-6903ab62-ca01-443d-bad2-48f0b7cd1c05"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Extraction of Clinically Relevant Temporal Gait Parameters from IMU Sensors Mimicking the Use of Smartphones","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-823587","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"This report presents the findings of a secondary analysis of the data from an RCT designed to compare salivary fluoride levels during 3 weeks of using 5000 or 1450-ppm fluoride toothpaste (n = 24/group). Following this trial phase, salivary fluoride monitoring continued for 2 weeks during which all 48 participants used 1450-ppm fluoride toothpaste in a wash-out phase. We investigated the temporal variability and longitudinal growth-based trajectories of salivary fluoride levels across study arms. During the trial phase, variability in the 5000-ppm arm exceeded that of the 1450-ppm arm. The best-fitting trajectories identified three clusters of participants following: low, medium or high salivary fluoride trajectories. Except for the 'high' cluster during the wash-out period, which featured only participants from the 5000-ppm trial arm, all clusters had participants from both trial arms. In conclusion, exploration of the variation and trajectories of salivary fluoride after 1450- and 5000-ppm fluoride toothpaste-use suggests non-constant responses to the initiation of and wash-out from the use of high-dosage fluoride toothpaste. This calls for the use of repeated period cross-over trial designs to investigate treatment effect heterogeneity. Reasons for and ways to minimize sources of individual variation in salivary fluoride levels are discussed.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Staun Larsen, Line","Bælum, Vibeke","Raittio, Eero"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-825461"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Finland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Eastern Finland"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"understanding the variation in salivary fluoride levels a secondary trajectory based analysis of trial data","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/eos.70039","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-825461","identifier":[{"id":"40908849","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-72663052-3f4a-4f69-b6c2-a2db9fe57350"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-FCAFEA84B326F1D1075570660B8B1CA1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/eos.70039","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University","Aarhus University, Health, Department of Dentistry and Oral Health, Department of Dentistry and Oral Health - Section for Oral Ecology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"fi","key":"university of eastern finland","loc":["Finland","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Eastern Finland","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Line","key":"staunlarsenpau","last":"Staun Larsen","locid":"35e2ae60-25db-4a15-893f-dc8906e2bccf","name":"Staun Larsen, Line","orcid":"0000-0002-4155-5884","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Vibeke","key":"baelumpau","last":"Baelum","locid":"fa28bbda-971e-4463-be61-3dcc358973b2","name":"Bælum, Vibeke","name_org":"Baelum, Vibeke","orcid":"0000-0002-2195-8053","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Eero Juhani","key":"raittiopau","last":"Raittio","locid":"c08351bf-55fc-4082-9e6e-cd59c6a6b513","name":"Raittio, Eero","name_org":"Raittio, Eero Juhani","orcid":"0000-0002-9258-9355","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40908849"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/eos.70039","issn":["09098836","16000722"],"issn_org":["16000722"],"journal":"European Journal of Oral Sciences Online","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell Munksgaard","status":"e","title":"European Journal of Oral Sciences Online","title_org":"European Journal of Oral Sciences"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["E-Published (ahead of print)"]},"sid":["au-72663052-3f4a-4f69-b6c2-a2db9fe57350"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Understanding the variation in salivary fluoride levels : A secondary trajectory-based analysis of trial data","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-825461","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Purpose: Treatment with glucocorticoids following paediatric cataract surgery is crucial to prevent inflammation, but may lead to secondary glaucoma, and hypothalamic–pituitary–adrenal axis suppression. We wish to compare glaucoma outcomes following high-dose and low-dose glucocorticoid treatment after paediatric cataract surgery. Methods: This cohort study included Danish children undergoing cataract surgery before 10 years of age, receiving either a low-dose or high-dose postoperative glucocorticoid treatment. Case identification and collection of a standardized dataset were retrospective, from 1 January 2010 to 31 December 2016, and prospective thereafter, until 31 December 2021. High-dose treatment included 0.5–1.0 mg subconjunctival depot dexamethasone or methylprednisolone, followed by 6–8 drops of dexamethasone for 1 week, tapered by one drop weekly. Low-dose treatment included 6 drops for 3 days, followed by 3 drops for 18 days. Sustained (>3 months) ocular hypertension or glaucoma was compared between the two groups. Results: Overall, 267 children (388 eyes) were included in the study. Ninety-five children (133 eyes) had received high-dose treatment and had a median follow-up time of 89 months (IQR: 57.2–107.4), while 173 children (255 eyes) had received the low-dose treatment and had a median follow-up time of 40.5 months (IQR: 22.9–60.4). Survival curves showed a lower risk of glaucoma in the low-dose group for children with axial lengths ≥18 mm. Conclusion: Low-dose glucocorticoid treatment was associated with a lower risk of glaucoma in children with axial lengths ≥18 mm. The same effect was not observed in children with shorter eyes. High-dose glucocorticoid should be limited in children undergoing cataract surgery.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Schmidt, Diana Chabané","Martinussen, Torben","Solebo, Ameenat Lola","Larsen, Dorte Ancher","Bach-Holm, Daniella","Kessel, Line"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-793237"],"filters":{"added":"2025-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Schmidt, Diana Chabané"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Great Ormond Street Hospital for Children NHS Foundation Trust","Great Ormond Street Hospital for Children NHS Trust","University College London"],"keyword":["Glaucoma","Glucocorticoid","Ocular hypertension","Steroid","congenital cataract","paediatric cataract","secondary glaucoma"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"comparing glaucoma risk in children receiving low dose and high dose glucocorticoid treatment after cataract surgery","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/aos.16746","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-793237","identifier":[{"id":"PMC11704827","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85201059292","type":"scopus"},{"id":"39132692","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-4d1b4ffa-bb5f-47f2-b540-a8868624ce87","ku-64e2e890-58f3-4699-b97c-3b6a4e9b1446"],"keyword":["congenital cataract","glaucoma","glucocorticoid","ocular hypertension","paediatric cataract","secondary glaucoma","steroid"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-657D086C4F2EE489AECBDF19FF88F1F8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/401854996/Acta_Ophthalmologica_2024_Schmidt_Comparing_glaucoma_risk_in_children_receiving_low_dose_and_high_dose_glucocorticoid.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/aos.16746","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Public Health, Section of Biostatistics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"university college london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University College London","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"gb","key":"great ormond street hospital for children nhs trust","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Great Ormond Street Hospital for Children NHS Trust","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"gb","key":"great ormond street hospital for children nhs foundation trust","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Great Ormond Street Hospital for Children NHS Foundation Trust","used":1},{"affno":"6","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Ophthalmology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"7","country":"dk","key":"central denmark region","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CDR","l3nl":"Central Denmark Region","l3nn":"Central Denmark Region","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Central Denmark Region","namevar":["Aarhus University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CDR","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Diana Chabané","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","name":"Schmidt, Diana Chabané","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Torben","key":"martinussenpau","last":"Martinussen","locid":"be649dfe-556d-49ca-9354-0507dbdaf7b5","name":"Martinussen, Torben","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4 5","first":"Ameenat Lola","key":"solebopau","last":"Solebo","name":"Solebo, Ameenat Lola","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"6 7","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Dorte Ancher","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"5e903371-0f65-473a-ad66-1a909c98a776","name":"Larsen, Dorte Ancher","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Daniella","key":"bachholmpau","last":"Bach-Holm","locid":"8b33b618-7a43-481a-a590-79dc9de2bc11","name":"Bach-Holm, Daniella","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Line","key":"kesselpau","last":"Kessel","locid":"3201472a-2f23-417e-bd01-a7950e2ff370","name":"Kessel, Line","orcid":"0000-0002-9375-1510","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["39132692"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/aos.16746","issn":["1755375X","17553768"],"issn_org":["1755375X"],"issue":"1","journal":"Acta Ophthalmologica","pages":"43-49","publisher":"John Wiley & Sons Inc.","status":"p","title":"Acta Ophthalmologica","volume":"103"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85201059292"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-4d1b4ffa-bb5f-47f2-b540-a8868624ce87","ku-64e2e890-58f3-4699-b97c-3b6a4e9b1446"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Comparing glaucoma risk in children receiving low-dose and high-dose glucocorticoid treatment after cataract surgery","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-793237","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Ålegræstransplantering og dyrkning af sukkertang har tidligere været foreslået som marine virkemidler til forbedring af miljøtilstanden i danske kystvande. I denne rapport præsenteres de væsentligste resultater og anvendte metoder fra projektet ”udviklingsinitiativer for marine virkemidler”. Projektets fokus har været at forbedre vidensgrundlaget for virkemidlerne med særligt fokus på deres anvendelsesområde i danske kystvande, kvantificering af miljøeffekter, metoder til håndtering af klimaeffekter samt den retlige ramme for anvendelsen af virkemidlerne. Nedenfor sammenfattes hovedresultaterne for hhv. ålegræs transplantering og dyrkning af tang. Ålegræstransplantering Ålegræstransplantering, hvor ålegræsskud høstes fra vilde donorbede og udplantes på egnet nøgenbund, har tidligere været beskrevet som et marint virkemiddel til immobilisering af næringsstoffer og til forbedring af miljøkvalitet (Timmermann et al. 2016; Bruhn et al., 2020, Petersen et al., 2021). Projektet om ”Udviklingsinitiativer for Marine Virkemidler” har ledt til ny viden om muligheder og effekter ved anvendelse af ålegræstransplantering. I dette afsnit præsenteres den opdaterede viden og data om ålegræs som marint virkemiddel i danske kystvande. Opsummering af resultater vedr. ålegræstransplantering som marint virkemiddel: Anvendelsespotentiale Under de nuværende miljømæssige forhold er transplantering af ålegræs begrænset af forhøjede næringsstofniveauer og afledte ændringer i miljøforhold, men i de fleste fjorde og kystvande vil der være områder, hvor fysiske, kemiske og biologiske forhold understøtter vækst af ålegræs og muliggør transplantering. Generelt er potentialer mindst i inderfjorde og større i yderfjorde og ved åbne kyststrækninger. Eksisterende ålegræs og nuværende fysiske aktiviteter og forhold ved kyster og på vandet kan yderligere begrænse det potentielt transplanteringsegnede areal betragteligt. In situ undersøgelser er påkrævet for alle lokaliteter før transplantering for at bestemme det reelle potentiale. Kvælstofreduktion (”N-virkemiddel”) Transplanteret ålegræs binder kvælstof, så det bliver utilgængeligt for fytoplankton. I den initiale tæthedsfase fra transplantering til steady state er opnået (maksimal ålegræstæthed på transplanteringsareal) sker binding af N i levende overjordisk og underjordisk biomasse samt i bladproduktionen. I denne fase, som ved BAT-design er estimeret til at vare 10 år, stiger N bindingsraten fra 0 til 65 kg N/ha/år. Efter steady state er det alene bladproduktionen som bidrager til en midlertidig N binding over vækstsæsonen. N bundet i afstødte blade vil blive frigivet over tid og dermed igen blive tilgængelig for fytoplankton. Den midlertidige N binding pga bladproduktion er estimeret til 59 kg N/ha/år i middel, og afhængig af omsætningsraten er den effektive N binding i afstødte blade således mellem 0 (total omsætning i vækstsæson) og 59 kg N/ha/år (ingen omsætning). Resultatet er opdateret ift. tidligere estimater for N-effekt ved ålegræstransplantering. Pga. stor variabilitet er det ikke muligt at kvantificere en eventuel netto N begravelse i ålegræssedimenter. Effekt i forhold til opnåelse af God Økologisk Tilstand (GØT) Ålegræs og andre rodfæstede blomsterplanter er et kvalitetselement i Vandrammedirektivet og transplantering kan være en nødvendig betingelse for reel opnåelse af GØT og/eller til hurtigere opnåelse af GØT pga. langsom naturlig spredning. Modelstudier viser, at transplantering af ålegræs på alle potentielt egnede arealer, lokalt kan reducere klorofyl og forbedre lysforholdene. I flere mindre vandområder er der betydelig effekt på vandområdeniveau. Effekt af ålegræs er i Vandområdeplanerne allerede indregnet i målbelastninger for kvælstof og indsatsbehov for de enkelte vandområder, idet det antages, at der ”straks” kommer ålegræs, når miljøforhold er egnede. Derfor kan en forventet miljøeffekt pga. ålegræstransplantering ikke umiddelbart omsættes til f.eks. en ændret målbelastning eller reduktionsbehov for kvælstof. Effekt ift. DK-klimaregnskab (”Klimavirkemiddel”) Ændring i C-binding i levende overjordisk og underjordisk ålegræs biomasse samt døde rødder kan medregnes som en ”klima” økosy-stemtjeneste, da det kan betragtes som en permanent og velbestemt C-lagring forårsaget af ålegræstransplantering. Når steady state i det transplanterede areal er opnået (maximal ålegræstæthed), er der ingen yderligere permanent C-binding. Med BAT-transplanteringsdesign kan tilvæksten fra tredje år tælles med, idet tætheden herefter kan antages > 10%. Fra tredje til tiende år (steady state) er C-bindingsraten i middel på 0,17 ton C/ha/år i 8 år, hvilket svarer til en samlet total C-binding på et transplanteret areal på 1,4 ton C/ha. Pga. stor variabilitet er det ikke muligt at kvantificere en eventuel netto C-begravelse i ålegræssedimenter. Andre miljøeffekter Der forekommer øget biodiversitet, målt som øget artsrigdom, tæthed og biomasse af in- og epifauna i transplanterede og naturlige åle-græsbede ift. nøgenbund. Tæthed og biomasse af fisk er også højere i (transplanterede) ålegræsbede. Økonomiske aspekter Omkostninger til transplantering af ålegræs omfatter omkostninger til hhv. forundersøgelser, selve transplanteringen og den efterfølgende monitering. Omkostningerne afhænger primært af i hvilken grad arbejdet udføres af frivillige eller professionelle, samt om der kræves en båd. Baseret på flere beregningsscenarier er omkostninger til selve transplanteringen estimeret til mellem 152 kkr/ha (uden båd, baseret på frivillige) til 872 kkr/ha (med båd, baseret på professionelle). Dertil kommer forundersøgelser som er estimeret til mellem 137 og 308 kkr/4 test-transekter samt omkostninger til den efterfølgende monitering, som er estimeret til mellem 30 og 145 kkr. Juridiske aspekter Ålegræs er central indikatorart iht. Vandrammedirektivet og flere af Habitat Direktivets naturtyper og der er en EU-retlig forpligtelse til aktivt at iværksætte de nødvendige initiativer til opnåelse af god økologisk tilstand/gunstig bevaringsstatus. Hertil kommer, at EU’s natur-genopretningsforordning, kræver genopretning af bl.a. havgræsenge. Ålegræstransplantering kan således ses som et led i opfyldelse af disse forpligtelser. Ålegræs som marint virkemiddel kan indgå som en supplerende foranstaltning i VRD indsatsprogrammer, men kan ikke træde i stedet for de grundlæggende foranstaltninger, herunder særligt begrænsning af udledningen af næringsstoffer. Tilladelser til ålegræsudplantning sker i praksis i henhold til kystbeskyttelseslovens § 16 a. Hjemmelsgrundlaget i kystbeskyttelsesloven for regulering af udplantning kan med fordel tydeliggøres. Der er ligeledes uklarhed angående retsgrundlaget for regulering af høst fra vilde ålegræsbestande, som er nødvendig forud for udplantning. Det bør derfor overvejes, om og på hvilken måde indsamling af ålegræs skal reguleres, f.eks. gennem et tilladelseskrav eller en generel regulering. En styrket områdebaseret beskyttelse af eksisterende forekomster af ålegræs og evt. potentielle ålegræsområder, bør ligeledes overvejes, ikke mindst i lyset af kravene i EU’s naturgenopretningsforordning. Dyrkning af sukkertang som marint virkemiddel Sukkertang (Saccharina latissima) er den makroalge (tang-art), der dyrkes mest af i Europa, hvor der samlet i 2019 blev dyrket og høstet ca. 376 ton vådvægt (VV) (Araújo et al. 2021). Sukkertang er en stor flerårig brunalge, der findes naturligt i alle danske havområder, men som trives bedst i farvande, hvor saliniteten er omkring eller over 20 PSU. Sukkertang kan dyrkes i havet på liner eller net, og dyrkningsteknologien er velbeskrevet og velafprøvet (Wegeberg 2010; Bruhn et al. 2016; Boderskov et al. 2021a; Boderskov et al. 2022). I Danmark har både kommercielle producenter og forskere dyrket sukkertang siden 2008, og der findes pt. tre danske kommercielle firmaer, der dyrker sukkertang. Sukkertang vokser ved fotosyntese, hvorved CO2 omsættes til organisk materiale og ilt ved hjælp af lysenergi, og tangen optager de nødvendige næringsstoffer (kvælstof, fosfor og spormetaller) fra det omgivende havvand. Kvælstof lagres (primært som protein) i sukkertangens væv, og bliver herved utilgængelig for andre organismer i havet, bl.a. planteplankton, så algeopblomstring mindskes. Når sukkertangen høstes, fjernes de næringsstoffer, der er optaget og bundet i tangbiomassen, fra havmiljøet. Sukkertangen kan efter høst anvendes til produktion af enten fødevarer, dyrefoder, materialer (f.eks. bioplast eller tekstilfibre), energi og/eller gødning, hvorved det bundne kvælstof kan genudnyt-tes og således understøtte en cirkulær bioøkonomi (Zhang et al. 2022). Opsummering af resultater vedr. tangdyrkning som virkemiddel Anvendelsespotentiale Dyrkning af sukkertang er mulig i alle danske marine vandområder. Ved brug af anlæg forudsættes en vanddybde > 3m. Den arealspecifikke væksthastighed - og dermed mængden af høstet biomasse - er højest, når salinitet, opløst uorganisk kvælstof (DIN) koncentration og lys er højest, hvilket giver størst arealspecifik vækst i det nordlige Jylland, inkl. Limfjorden og langs Jyllands østkyst. Nuværende aktiviteter, som ikke er forenelige med tangdyrkning, kan begrænse det potentielt anvendelige areal betydeligt. Derudover kan miljøhensyn (f.eks. beskyttelse af ålegræs) begrænse det anvendelige areal til tangdyrkning. Kvælstofreduktion (”N-virkemiddel”) Makroalger binder kvælstof i vækstfasen, som fjernes når tangen høstes. Den arealspecifikke kvælstoffjernelse ved høst er meget variabel og afhænger af vækstpotentiale, DIN-koncentration og dyrkningsmetode. Ved optimale miljøforhold og nuværende dyrkningsmetode (anno 2023), kan tangdyrkning fjerne op til 23 kg N/ha/år, ved 1000 m line pr. ha. Indenfor 5-10 år må dette forventes at kunne 5-dobles i takt med, at metoder til at øge linetætheden i anlæggene udvikles. Effekt i forhold til opnåelse af God Økologisk Tilstand (GØT) I dette studie med fokus på Limfjorden har modellering vist at tangdyrkning i realistiske opskaleringsscenarier ikke påvirker WFD-indikatorerne ”sommer klorofyl” og ”lys i vækstsæsonen for fytoplankton” og dermed ingen effekt har på disse specifikke biologiske kvalitetselementer og opnåelse af GØT. En medvirkende årsag er, at GØT-parametrene måles udenfor dyrkningssæsonen af sukkertang. Effekt ift. DK-klima-regnskab (”Klimavirkemiddel”) Tangdyrkning kan ikke umiddelbart indgå i det nationale klimagas-emissionsregnskab, idet der ikke sker en permanent lagring af kulstof af den høstede biomasse. Lagring af kulstof ved permanent begravelse i sediment vurderes som værende marginal og usikker og er ikke kvantificeret. Anvendt som fødevare, foder eller materiale kan tang fortrænge produkter med højere CO2-aftryk og dermed tælle positivt i Livs Cyklus Analyser (LCA). Andre miljøeffekter Dyrkning af sukkertang har effekt på strømforhold og stratificering af vandsøjlen indenfor tanganlægget ved høj biomasse, og kan bidrage til kanaliseret flow og derfor ændret sedimentation lokalt. I tanganlægget blev der periodevis observeret højere pH og områder med øget lyssvækkelse til bunden (Kd), men ændringerne var ikke entydige pga. høj omrøring og vandudskiftning, som medførte en betydelig daglig variation. Tanganlægget forårsagede ikke øget omsætning eller iltforbrug i sedimentet. Økonomiske aspekter Omkostninger relateret til dyrkning af sukkertang omfatter dyrkningsanlæg, klargøring af sporeliner, drift og høst. Den årlige omkostning til afskrivning af anlæg og for at producere sukkertang på et standardanlæg i Limfjorden (18 ha) er anslået til 1,16 million Kr, svarende til 64 Kr/ha/år. Prisen for at producere sukkertang er således beregnet til 12,63 Kr/kg vådvægt (VV) og 8,34 Kr/m spireline produceret, og prisen for fjernelse af kvælstof vil således svare til 2.800 Kr pr. kg kvælstof høstet ved Sallingsund i Limfjorden. Omkostningsestimater er baseret på en timeløn på 250 kt/time og forudsætter en 10-årig driftsperiode til afskrivning af anlægget. Juridiske aspekter Etablering af tanganlæg som marint virkemiddel kan formentlig indgå som en supplerende foranstaltning i vandrammedirektivets indsatsprogrammer. De supplerende foranstaltninger kan dog ikke træde i stedet for de grundlæggende foranstaltninger i vandrammedirektivet, herunder særligt begrænsning af udledningen af næringsstoffer. Tilladelser til tangdyrkning sker i henhold til kystbeskyttelseslovens § 16 a. Her er det væsentligt at sikre, at der i de konkrete tilladelser foretages en grundig belysning af de konkrete miljøforhold forbundet med projektet, både de eksisterende forhold og projektets mulige miljøpåvirkning, bl.a. i forhold til Natura 2000-områder og miljømål. Yderligere kan det bemærkes, at retsgrundlaget for høst af vild tang er uklart, og ny regulering bør overvejes. Det kan eventuelt ske i sammenhæng med en styrket beskyttelse af værdifulde forekomster af vild tang, , bl.a. i lyset af kravene i EU’s naturgenopretningsforordning om genopretning og beskyttelse af visse makroalgeskove.","author_count":38,"contributors":["Timmermann, Karen","Mostovaya, Alina","Barnewitz, Anders","Erichsen, Anders","Petersen, Anders H.","Holbach, Andreas","Bruhn, Annette","Nielsen, Benjamin","Taylor, Daniel","Hansen, Flemming Thorbjørn","Hansen, Frederik H.","Christiansen, Freja","Anker, Helle Tegner","Christensen, Jesper","Holding, Johnna","Ehrenreich, Julie Lyhne","Baaner, Lasse","Hermannsen, Line","Callisen, Lærke W.","Nielsen, Mette Møller","Gommesen, Mia","Lees, Mikkel K.","Flindt, Mogens R.","Holst, Niels","Canal-Vergés, Paula","Schmedes, Peter Søndergaard","Kjær, Rasmus A.","Steinfurth, Rune C. H.","Stæhr, Sanjina Upadhyay","Pedersen, Sidsel Gurholt","Vinbæk, Sidsel Juul","Gyldenkærne, Steen","Andersen, Søren Stig","Boderskov, Teis","Jørgensen, Thor S.","Banke, Timi L.","Andersen, Trine A.","Larsen, Trine C."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-827804"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"11-50