"name_org":"Loos, Ruth","orcid":"0000-0002-8532-5087","rank":20,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Torben","key":"hansentorbenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"6b6bfff1-8b14-468b-af77-e71b375018e0","name":"Hansen, Torben","orcid":"0000-0001-8748-3831","rank":21,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","first":"Allan","key":"linnebergallanpau","last":"Linneberg","locid":"09da8aa6-fd95-4ace-a2f1-19f38d7d3f9e","name":"Linneberg, Allan","orcid":"0000-0002-0994-0184","rank":22,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3 5 6","first":"Allan","key":"vaagallanpau","last":"Vaag","locid":"4b68020d-63cf-4701-ad10-cbc4845d0f1c","name":"Vaag, Allan","rank":23,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38286704"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1136/bmjopen-2023-078501","issn":["20446055"],"issue":"1","journal":"BMJ Open","paperid":"e078501","status":"p","title":"BMJ Open","volume":"14"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85184011808"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-a7f818c8-f801-44f3-9713-f89c44ed44c9"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Protocol for the combined cardiometabolic deep phenotyping and registry-based 20-year follow-up study of the Inter99 cohort","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-721417","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Time-domain induced polarization (TDIP) data are often perturbed by undesired electrical and electromagnetic responses, i.e., powerline harmonics, spikes etc., which along with the random noise from the instrument mask the measured IP data. This limits access to the spectral content, which is vital for characterizing the electrical properties of rocks, soil and minerals in the subsurface. Therefore, mitigating noise responses is key to gaining access to the spectral content. To this end, existing literature assumes prior noise models to deal with the harmonic, spike, and random noises whereby errors in the estimated model parameters can leave residuals, which are significant enough to question the reliability of the inversion results. To address this problem, we employ variational mode decomposition (VMD) that uses a data driven approach to decompose a TDIP signal into its intrinsic oscillatory modes, i.e., amplitude modulated-frequency modulated (AM-FM) sinusoids. The optimization based decomposition framework in VMD dynamically adjusts filtering properties according to the nature of the input TDIP signal. Since IP decays and noise have different spectral properties, we employ a multi-stage VMD operation to gradually isolate the signal of interest from noise. Through simulations and field examples we demonstrate that the proposed VMD based approach enhances the reconstruction of the IP decay and hence improves its inversion and interpretation.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Naveed, Khuram","Meldgaard Madsen, Line","Grombacher, Denys","Larsen, Jakob Juul"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-727314"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Current measurement","Harmonic analysis","IP networks","Logic gates","Noise measurement","Power harmonic filters","Time domain induced polarization TDIP","Variational mode decomposition VMD","intrinsic mode function IMF"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"processing time domain induced polarization data using variational mode decomposition","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85184818637","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-727314","identifier":[{"id":"85184818637","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-50202bc7-bc61-4cc5-8369-3b53106fb0dd"],"keyword":["Current measurement","Full waveform IP decay","Full waveform induced polarization (IP) decay","Geophysical measurements","Harmonic analysis","IP networks","Intrinsic mode function (IMF)","Logic gates","Noise measurement","Power harmonic filters","Time-domain induced polarization (TDIP)","Variational mode decomposition (VMD)","time-domain IP (TDIP)"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-F1881A2F3774DAE5D14410C5086D8E43"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering - Signal Processing and Machine learning"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Khuram","key":"naveedpau","last":"Naveed","locid":"4f58e982-d7d5-4523-b0f6-a5c4850686b8","name":"Naveed, Khuram","orcid":"0000-0001-8286-6139","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Line Meldgaard","key":"madsenpau","last":"Madsen","locid":"9e5e14d1-c032-4469-bde5-e1fba2710bf2","name":"Meldgaard Madsen, Line","name_org":"Madsen, Line Meldgaard","orcid":"0000-0001-5435-9914","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Denys","key":"grombacherpau","last":"Grombacher","locid":"4abc41e2-38a9-4496-8861-7c076cc891f1","name":"Grombacher, Denys","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jakob Juul","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"3606236a-56cc-44ea-9024-093ba943b481","name":"Larsen, Jakob Juul","orcid":"0000-0002-4509-4480","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/tgrs.2024.3362290","issn":["01962892","15580644"],"issn_org":["01962892"],"journal":"IEEE Transactions on Geoscience and Remote Sensing","pages":"1-13","paperid":"5905713","publisher":"Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.","status":"p","title":"IEEE Transactions on Geoscience and Remote Sensing","volume":"62"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85184818637"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-50202bc7-bc61-4cc5-8369-3b53106fb0dd"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Processing Time-domain Induced Polarization Data Using Variational Mode Decomposition","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-727314","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"INTRODUCTION: The aim of this study is to investigate the integration of movement and physical activity (MoPA) within Early Childhood Teacher Education (ECTE) policies across Denmark, Finland, Iceland, Norway, and Sweden. This knowledge can inform the development of ECTE policies and practices that promote MoPA in Early Childhood Education and Care (ECEC) in Nordic countries and other countries worldwide. METHODS: In this study, a Nordic cross-national network of researchers collaborated in investigating policy documents at the national and university levels, which govern the education of ECEC teachers. This study was inspired by the Non-affirmative Theory of Education, which provides a framework for understanding the various influences on curricular development in higher education. Based on this, a four-step comparative analytical process of national and university documents across the Nordic countries was conducted. It included keyword search for MoPA related courses and a qualitative description of MoPA in ECTE. Thus, a combination of investigations of policy documents at the national and university level and expert knowledge set a solid foundation for international comparison. RESULTS: The comparative analysis of MoPA in ECTE reveals diverse approaches influenced by national and university policies. A central theme is the variability in MoPA integration across these nations. Finland and Norway prioritize MoPA with independent mandatory courses. In Iceland, compulsory MoPA courses exist at one of two universities, and in Sweden at three out of 19. All university colleges in Denmark offer an elective course. Furthermore, learning objectives related to MoPA are, to varying degrees, part of the internships in the countries, with Sweden being an exception. In the participating countries, the teachers decide the content of the MoPA courses with little guidance, support, and agreement on essential MoPA content within and across the ECTE's. Norway has established guidelines, and in Finland, there is a network of ECTE Physical Education (PE) educators, which, to some degree, increases the consistency and quality of MoPA in education. DISCUSSION: The Nordic countries present diverse MoPA integration approaches rooted in national policies and educational traditions. The findings emphasize the necessity of independent and mandatory MoPA courses, integration of MoPA into internships and promoting networks across the educational and academic sectors to equip future early childhood educators with competencies for fostering physical activity, motor development and children's well-being.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Christiansen, Lars Breum","Ekberg, Jan-Eric","Soini, Anne","Larsen, Robert","Kristjánsdóttir, Gudrún","Froberg, Karsten","Sollerhed, Ann-Christin","Sääkslahti, Arja","Fjørtoft, Ingunn","Vilhjálmsson, Rúnar","Olesen, Line Grønholt"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-740794"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Christiansen, Lars Breum"],"country":["Finland","Iceland","Norway","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Kristianstad University","Malmö University","University of Iceland","University of Jyväskylä","University of South-Eastern Norway"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CDR","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a comparative analysis of movement and physical activity in early childhood teacher education policy in five nordic countries","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fspor.2024.1352520","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-740794","