":"0000-0001-8901-7850","rank":26,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38801970"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.ijid.2024.107111","issn":["12019712","18783511"],"issn_org":["12019712"],"journal":"International Journal of Infectious Diseases","paperid":"107111","publisher":"Elsevier BV","publisher_org":"Elsevier B.V.","status":"p","title":"International Journal of Infectious Diseases","volume":"146"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85196316682"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","North Denmark Region","Region Zealand","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-ebb35e57-082c-48bf-a97d-04202c86af1f","au-3c5c57c1-7a5f-45db-be00-cb9344b49615","ku-17a3fa9e-b2a8-416f-a762-fa10ee30e24c","sdu-8dd92032-b7a8-4e7b-b8ab-5ffe6e27950d"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Development of antibody levels and subsequent decline in individuals with vaccine induced and hybrid immunity to SARS-CoV-2","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S04-095.89-741595","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Background and Hypotheses: Impaired executive control is a potential prognostic and endophenotypic marker of schizophrenia (SZ) and bipolar disorder (BP). Assessing children with familial high-risk (FHR) of SZ or BP enables characterization of early risk markers and we hypothesize that they express impaired executive control as well as aberrant brain activation compared to population-based control (PBC) children. Study Design: Using a flanker task, we examined executive control together with functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) in 11- to 12-year-old children with FHR of SZ (FHR-SZ) or FHR of BP (FHR-BP) and PBC children as part of a register-based, prospective cohort-study; The Danish High Risk and Resilience study—VIA 11. Study Results: We included 85 (44% female) FHR-SZ, 63 (52% female) FHR-BP and 98 (50% female) PBC in the analyses. Executive control effects, caused by the spatial visuomotor conflict, showed no differences between groups. Bayesian ANOVA of reaction time (RT) variability, quantified by the coefficient of variation (CVRT), revealed a group effect with similarly higher CVRT in FHR-BP and FHR-SZ compared to PBC (BF10 = 6.82). The fMRI analyses revealed no evidence for between-group differences in task-related brain activation. Post hoc analyses excluding children with psychiatric illness yielded same results. Conclusion: FHR-SZ and FHR-BP at age 11–12 show intact ability to resolve a spatial visuomotor conflict and neural efficacy. The increased variability in RT may reflect difficulties in maintaining sustained attention. Since variability in RT was independent of existing psychiatric illness, it may reflect a potential endophenotypic marker of risk.","author_count":25,"contributors":["Johnsen, Line Korsgaard","Larsen, Kit Melissa","Fuglsang, Søren Asp","Ver Loren van Themaat, Anna Hester","Baaré, William Frans Christiaan","Madsen, Kathrine Skak","Madsen, Kristoffer Hougaard","Hemager, Nicoline","Andreassen, Anna Krogh","Veddum, Lotte","Greve, Aja Neergaard","Nejad, Ayna Baladi","Burton, Birgitte Klee","Gregersen, Maja","Eichele, Heike","Lund, Torben E","Bliksted, Vibeke","Thorup, Anne Amalie Elgaard","Mors, Ole","Plessen, Kerstin Jessica","Nordentoft, Merete","Siebner, Hartwig Roman"],"cuid":["S04-096.57-735349"],"filters":{"added":"2023-11","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":4,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD","Oceania"],"corresponding_author":["Siebner, Hartwig Roman"],"country":["Australia","Germany","Norway","Switzerland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Department of Neurology, University Hospital Münster, Germany","Haukeland University Hospital","Lausanne University Hospital","Lausanne University Hospital (CHUV)","The Joanna Briggs Institute, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, The University of Adelaide, Adelaide, Australia.","University of Lausanne"],"keyword":["BOLD","Bipolar Disorder physiopathology","Child","Cohort study","Denmark","Endophenotype","Executive Function physiology","Female","Flanker task","Functional magnetic resonance imaging","Genetic predisposition","Humans","Magnetic Resonance Imaging","Male","Neurocognitive functioning","Prospective Studies","Registries","Risk","Schizophrenia physiopathology"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-NP","DI-PR","DI-U","DI-UC"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-ZEA"],"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-LUND"],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-NOVO"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"UC":["DI-UC-ALL","DI-UC-KP"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-NP-LUND","DI-PR-NOVO","DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU","DI-UC-KP"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"executive control and associated brain activity in children with familial high risk of schizophrenia or bipolar disorder a danish register based study","src":["au","dtu","kp","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU","DI-UC-KP"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/schbul/sbad134","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This study was funded by The Lundbeck Foundation Initiative for Integrative Psychiatric Research - iPSYCH (grant number R248-2017-2003) and Innovation Fund Denmark (grant number 6152-00002B). Additionally, LKJ was funded by The Independent Research Fund (grant number DFF - 6110-00139), Denmark, and KML received funding from Mental Health services\\u2014Capital Region of Denmark (no grant number given) as well as the Lundbeck Foundation (grant number R322-2019-2311). Hartwig R. Siebner holds a 5-year professorship in precision medicine at the Faculty of Health Sciences and Medicine, University of Copenhagen which is sponsored by the Lundbeck Foundation (grant number R186-2015-2138). The funders had no role in study design, data collection, data analysis, data interpretation, decision to publish or preparation of the manuscript."],"id":"S04-096.57-735349","identifier":[{"id":"PMC11059809","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85191760744","type":"scopus"},{"id":"37756493","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-efa594e3-d2c9-499a-85ba-9e29a267da73","dtu-a1e7b8d0-650f-4263-9526-d70f0d42665f","kp-41a4a22a-9c8f-4d2a-a423-66275d643b2b","ku-638e3da2-cc0a-418b-adf5-3cba4d334827"],"keyword":["BOLD","Bipolar Disorder/physiopathology","Child","Cohort study","Denmark","Endophenotype","Executive Function/physiology","Female","Flanker task","Functional magnetic resonance imaging","Genetic predisposition","Humans","Magnetic Resonance Imaging","Male","Neurocognitive functioning","Prospective Studies","Registries","Risk","Schizophrenia/physiopathology","Task-related BOLD response","Visuomotor response conflict","functional magnetic resonance imaging/task-related","predisposition/endophenotype/cohort study","response conflict/flanker","response/visuomotor"