pital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-NP","DI-NP-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","DI-UC","DI-UC-ALL","Hospitals","KP","Non-Profit Organisations","Universities","University Colleges","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-02947b59-e909-485f-be2b-1d9341b998ed","kp-0cf49bff-4874-41ca-8ef7-9ad23027d041","ku-b35d9428-e97f-4234-997b-577c35349cb3"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KP University College Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Danish High-Risk and Resilience Study-VIA 15 - A Study Protocol for the Third Clinical Assessment of a Cohort of 522 Children Born to Parents Diagnosed With Schizophrenia or Bipolar Disorder and Population-Based Controls","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-095.44-012782","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"PURPOSE: To examine the incidence of mental disorders in children with cataract compared with children without cataract. DESIGN: Nationwide cohort study based on entries in comprehensive national databases. METHODS: The incidence of mental disorders in children born between 2000 and 2017 diagnosed with cataract before 10 years of age (n = 485) was compared with sex- and age-matched controls (n = 4358). Analyses were corrected to somatic disease in the child and parental socioeconomic status and psychiatric morbidity. The study was conducted as 2 university hospitals in Denmark managing children 6 years of age our younger with cataract. RESULTS: The incidence of mental disorders was nearly doubled in children with cataract compared with controls (odds ratio [OR], 1.83; 95% CI, 1.28–3.63). The risk of anxiety disorders was quadrupled (OR, 4.10; 95% CI, 1.90–8.84) and the risk of developmental delay was doubled (OR, 2.66; 95% CI, 1.45–4.90). The risk of mental disorders was significantly higher in children diagnosed with cataract in the first 3 years of life compared with controls (OR, 2.36; 95% CI, 1.53–3.64), whereas those diagnosed with cataract later in childhood did not have an increased risk (OR, 1.24; 95% CI, 0.66–2.30). CONCLUSIONS: The risk of mental disorders, in particular anxiety and neurodevelopmental delay, is markedly increased in children with cataract and even more so in those diagnosed within the first 3 years of life. Psychiatric screening instruments may be integrated in the management of these children.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Al-Bakri, Moug","Skovgaard, Anne Mette","Bach-Holm, Daniella","Larsen, Dorte Ancher","Siersma, Volkert","Kessel, Line"],"cuid":["S03-100.00-023027"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"corresponding_author":["Al-Bakri, Moug"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"increased incidence of mental disorders in children with cataract findings from a population based study","src":["au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ajo.2021.09.034","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Funding/Support: The study received financial support from Øjenforeningen (Fight for Sight Denmark) and Carl and Nicoline Larsen´s Fund, Synoptik Foundation and Øjenfonden. The funding bodies had no influence on conception, design or reporting of the study. None of the authors have any conflicts of interests to declare."],"id":"S03-100.00-023027","identifier":[{"id":"85122005868","type":"scopus"},{"id":"34648774","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-8e3cd5f6-4f6e-4a0e-a9c0-fd6597118ce5","ku-9f8b0c77-36ac-473c-b6b6-21a0ee392bf9","sdu-c87f7e98-5dc0-4403-95cf-3c56fe6f6ec6"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-45FF584F218D4416FF1E832051E42D17"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/289394149/1_s2.0_S0002939421004992_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/197387590/1_s2.0_S0002939421004992_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ajo.2021.09.034","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/352045639/Increased_Incidence_of_Mental_Disorders_in_Children_with_Cataract.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["M