, Anne","rank":16,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"IEH","key":"madsenidaehpau","last":"Madsen","locid":"7bad9fdb-4af4-46fa-846a-9a16db2a2419","name":"Madsen, Ida E H","name_org":"Madsen, IEH","orcid":"0000-0003-3635-3900","rank":17,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"R.","key":"ruguliesreinerpau","last":"Rugulies","locid":"4684d49a-5fe6-43e0-b3a6-5959f3d2f472","name":"Rugulies, Reiner","name_org":"Rugulies, R.","orcid":"0000-0002-7752-131X","rank":18,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["988779043749"],"place":"København","publisher":"Det Nationale Forskningscenter for Arbejdsmiljø","repno":"NFA-rapport","series":"NFA-rapport","status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","Governmental Institutions"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["nfa-8c045335-6161-493c-85f7-eaf4df7d715e","nfa-d6d47e39-0f95-4b03-a3ad-2dab111f2dd6"],"src":["NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment"],"title":"Litteraturgennemgang med supplerende analyser af årsagerne til ufrivillig, førtidig tilbagetrækning fra arbejdsmarkedet. Appendiks til afslutningsrapport til Arbejdsmiljøforskningsfonden","type":"Report","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-087.36-769532","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Children, adolescents, and young adults with at least one first-degree relative [familial high-risk (FHR)] with either schizophrenia (SZ) or bipolar disorder (BD) have a one-in-two risk of developing a psychiatric disorder. Here, we review functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) studies which examined task-related brain activity in young individuals with FHR-SZ and FHR-BD. A systematic search identified all published task-related fMRI studies in children, adolescents, and young adults below an age of 27 years with a first-degree relative with SZ or BD, but without manifest psychotic or affective spectrum disorder themselves. The search identified 19 cross-sectional fMRI studies covering four main cognitive domains: 1) working memory (n = 3), 2) cognitive control (n = 4), 3) reward processing (n = 3), and 4) emotion processing (n = 9). Thirteen studies included FHR-BD, five studies included FHR-SZ, and one study included a pooled FHR group. In general, task performance did not differ between the respective FHR groups and healthy controls, but 18 out of the 19 fMRI studies revealed regional alterations in task-related activation. Brain regions showing group differences in peak activation were regions associated with the respective task domain and showed little overlap between FHR-SZ and FHR-BD. The low number of studies, together with the low number of subjects, and the substantial heterogeneity of employed methodological approaches within the domain of working memory, cognitive control, and reward processing impedes finite conclusions. Emotion processing was the most investigated task domain in FHR-BD. Four studies reported differences in activation of the amygdala, and two studies reported differences in activation of inferior frontal/middle gyrus. Together, these studies provide evidence for altered brain processing of emotions in children, adolescents, and young adults at FHR-BD. More studies of higher homogeneity, larger sample sizes and with a longitudinal study design are warranted to prove a shared or specific FHR-related endophenotypic brain activation in young first-degree relatives of individuals with SZ or BD, as well as to pinpoint specific alterations in brain activation during cognitive-, emotional-, and reward-related tasks.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Korsgaard Johnsen, Line","Ver Loren van Themaat, Anna Hester","Larsen, Kit Melissa","Burton, Birgitte Klee","Baaré, William Frans Christiaan","Madsen, Kathrine Skak","Nordentoft, Merete","Siebner, Hartwig Roman","Plessen, Kerstin Jessica"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-038760"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Switzerland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Lausanne University Hospital"],"keyword":["Adolescents","Bipolar disorder","Children","Familial high risk","Neurocognitive function","Schizophrenia","fMRI functional magnetic resonance imaging"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U","DI-UC"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-KEN"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"],"UC":["DI-UC-ALL","DI-UC-KP"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-KEN","DI-U-KU","DI-UC-KP"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"alterations in task related brain activation in children adolescents and young adults at familial high risk for schizophrenia or bipolar disorder a systematic review","src":["kp","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-UC-KP"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyt.2020.00632","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-038760","identifier":[{"id":"85088787391","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC7365908","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"32754058","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["kp-3fb23fa2-2b8f-4793-b991-27658879883d","ku-29ea3629-4f24-4ad1-823e-ced6cef83cc5"],"keyword":["adolescents","bipolar disorder","children","fMRI—functional magnetic resonance imaging","familial high-risk","neurocognitive function","schizophrenia"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-B338B4F99D5C9C6338DB8D7DA9806649"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyt.2020.00632","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/250978512/fpsyt_11_00632.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fpsyt.2020.00632/full","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"kennedy center","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-KEN","l3nn":"Kennedy Center","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Kennedy Center","namevar":["Kennedy Center"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-KEN","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Amager-Hvidovre Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"kp university college copenhagen","l2":"DI-UC","l2n":"University Colleges","l3":"DI-UC-KP","l3nl":"KP","l3nn":"KP University College Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KP University College Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Professionshøjskole, Radiografuddannelsen, Radiografuddannelsen"],"nora_code":"DI-UC-KP","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Line Korsgaard","key":"johnsenpau","last":"Johnsen","locid":"b9ee691d-486a-49ce-b35a-766d5dc2daba","name":"Korsgaard Johnsen, Line","name_org":"Johnsen, Line Korsgaard","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Anna Hester","key":"verlorenvanthemaatpau","last":"Ver Loren van Themaat","name":"Ver Loren van Themaat, Anna Hester","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Kit Melissa","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Kit Melissa","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Birgitte Klee","key":"burtonpau","last":"Burton","name":"Burton, Birgitte Klee","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"William Frans Christiaan","key":"baarépau","last":"Baaré","name":"Baaré, William Frans Christiaan","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Kathrine Skak","key":"madsenpau","last":"Madsen","locid":"69fff7ae-0b8c-4fc1-8c30-a29e12eb7f0c","name":"Madsen, Kathrine Skak","orcid":"0000-0001-8884-5902","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Merete","key":"nordentoftpau","last":"Nordentoft","locid":"6d0fe19c-7c81-463b-8bc8-822d71584b69","name":"Nordentoft, Merete","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Hartwig Roman","key":"siebnerpau","last":"Siebner","locid":"d9ed8669-a16b-429a-86ed-353ef481bb72","name":"Siebner, Hartwig Roman","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Kerstin Jessica","key":"plessenpau","last":"Plessen","name":"Plessen, Kerstin Jessica","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["32754058"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3389/fpsyt.2020.00632","issn":["16640640"],"journal":"Frontiers in Psychiatry","paperid":"632","publisher":"Frontiers Media SA","status":"p","title":"Frontiers in Psychiatry","volume":"11"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85088787391"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","All University Colleges","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","DI-UC","DI-UC-ALL","Hospitals","KP","Universities","University Colleges","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["kp-3fb23fa2-2b8f-4793-b991-27658879883d","ku-29ea3629-4f24-4ad1-823e-ced6cef83cc5"],"src":["KP University College Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Alterations in Task-Related Brain Activation in Children, Adolescents and Young Adults at Familial High-Risk for Schizophrenia or Bipolar Disorder - A Systematic Review","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-038760","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"The genetic architecture of the small and isolated Greenlandic population is advantageous for identification of novel genetic variants associated with cardio-metabolic traits. We aimed to identify genetic loci associated with body mass index (BMI), to expand the knowledge of the genetic and biological mechanisms underlying obesity. Stage 1 BMI-association analyses were performed in 4,626 Greenlanders. Stage 2 replication and meta-analysis were performed in additional cohorts comprising 1,058 Yup'ik Alaska Native people, and 1,529 Greenlanders. Obesity-related traits were assessed in the stage 1 study population. We identified a common variant on chromosome 11, rs4936356, where the derived G-allele had a frequency of 24% in the stage 1 study population. The derived allele was genome-wide significantly associated with lower BMI (beta (SE), -0.14 SD (0.03), p = 3.2x10-8), corresponding to 0.64 kg/m2 lower BMI per G allele in the stage 1 study population. We observed a similar effect in the Yup'ik cohort (-0.09 SD, p = 0.038), and a non-significant effect in the same direction in the independent Greenlandic stage 2 cohort (-0.03 SD, p = 0.514). The association remained genome-wide significant in meta-analysis of the Arctic cohorts (-0.10 SD (0.02), p = 4.7x10-8). Moreover, the variant was associated with a leaner body type (weight, -1.68 (0.37) kg; waist circumference, -1.52 (0.33) cm; hip circumference, -0.85 (0.24) cm; lean mass, -0.84 (0.19) kg; fat mass and percent, -1.66 (0.33) kg and -1.39 (0.27) %; visceral adipose tissue, -0.30 (0.07) cm; subcutaneous adipose tissue, -0.16 (0.05) cm, all p<0.0002), lower insulin resistance (HOMA-IR, -0.12 (0.04), p = 0.00021), and favorable lipid levels (triglyceride, -0.05 (0.02) mmol/l, p = 0.025; HDL-cholesterol, 0.04 (0.01) mmol/l, p = 0.0015). In conclusion, we identified a novel variant, where the derived G-allele possibly associated with lower BMI in Arctic populations, and as a consequence also leaner body type, lower insulin resistance, and a favorable lipid profile.","author_count":24,"contributors":["Andersen, Mette K.","Jørsboe, Emil","Skotte, Line","Hanghøj, Kristian","Sandholt, Camilla Helene","Moltke, Ida","Grarup, Niels","Kern, Timo","Mahendran, Yuvaraj","Søborg, Bolette","Bjerregaard, Peter","Larsen, Christina V. L.","Dahl-Petersen, Inger K.","Tiwari, Hemant K.","Feenstra, Bjarke","Koch, Anders","Wiener, Howard W.","Hopkins, Scarlett E.","Pedersen, Oluf","Melbye, Mads","Boyer, Bert B.","Jørgensen, Marit E.","Albrechtsen, Anders","Hansen, Torben"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-043370"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Germany","Greenland","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Grønlands Universitet","Oregon Health & Science University","PEPperPRINT GmbH","Stanford University","University of Alabama at Birmingham","University of Alaska"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI"],"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-SSI","DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the derived allele of a novel intergenic variant at chromosome 000000000011 associates with lower body mass index and a favorable metabolic phenotype in greenlanders","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pgen.1008544","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-043370","identifier":[{"id":"85079079642","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC7001991","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"31978080","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-70458cef-e154-4437-8804-f1e1a508e0a0","sdu-2a2f9b64-634c-48ce-ad53-9032984e118a"],"lang":"