de":"pau"},{"affno":"16","corresponding":null,"first":"Yvonne","key":"weberpau","last":"Weber","name":"Weber, Yvonne","rank":23,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"17","corresponding":null,"first":"Peter M.","key":"krawitzpau","last":"Krawitz","name":"Krawitz, Peter M.","rank":24,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"13 18","corresponding":null,"first":"Ingo","key":"helbigpau","last":"Helbig","name":"Helbig, Ingo","rank":25,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"19","corresponding":null,"first":"Usha","key":"kinipau","last":"Kini","name":"Kini, Usha","rank":26,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rikke S.","key":"møllerpau","last":"Møller","locid":"85de2fa9-456c-4427-8c30-7dc1bdd4bcf0","name":"Møller, Rikke S.","orcid":"0000-0002-9664-1448","rank":27,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["30976099"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41436-019-0512-3","issn":["10983600","15300366"],"issn_org":["10983600"],"issue":"10","journal":"Genetics in Medicine","pages":"2216-2223","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Genetics in Medicine","volume":"21"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85064261102"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Private Research","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Other Companies","Private Research","Universities","University of Southern Denmark","amplexa genetics a/s"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-90d835c1-e0fc-421f-a4b2-48a53de3f297"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"PIGT-CDG, a disorder of the glycosylphosphatidylinositol anchor : description of 13 novel patients and expansion of the clinical characteristics","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-614702","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Introduction Assessment of balance is key to identifying individuals with postural control deficits and an increased fall risk. Subjects may compensate for their deficits by utilizing other strategies; to avoid this, it is recommended to assess postural control using a dual-task test. In most dual-task tests, it is difficult to monitor the performance in the secondary task and the individual’s task prioritisation. This study evaluated a new dual-task testing approach. Materials and methods A convenience sample of 54 community-dwelling elderly (age 65+ years) and a reference group of 20 young participants were included in the study. They performed a test in which they could utilize cues to improve their baseline performance, provided their level of postural control allowed them residual attention capacity for this cognitive task. Results Significant performance differences were seen between the young and the elderly. The young group improved their performance time by 23.9% (10.7) and 7.1% (14.2) with a cue and a reverse cue, respectively, whereas the elderly failed to improve their performance time. The test was unable to distinguish between individuals within the elderly group due to a floor effect. Discussion The test reveals an individual’s capacity to use cues for anticipatory postural control strategies in a dual-task setting and thereby estimates automatization of postural control. While the young subjects were capable of improving their performance during dual-task conditions, the elderly subjects apparently had no residual attentional capacity allowing them to utilize the facilitating cues. Within the elderly group, the dual-task aspects of the test added no value with respect to differentiation in the level of postural control.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Læssøe, Uffe","Larsen, Camilla Bille","Schunck, Line Noerkjaer","Lehmann, Line Jensen","Iversen, Halla"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-650036"],"filters":{"added":"2023-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Physiotherapy"],"level":"sci","oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-UC"],"level3":{"UC":["DI-UC-ALL","DI-UC-UCN"]},"level3all":["DI-UC-UCN"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"age related differences in balance approached by a novel dual task test of anticipatory postural control strategies","src":["ucn"],"src-code":["DI-UC-UCN"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0218371","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-650036","identifier":[{"id":"85068988822","type":"scopus"},{"id":"31246971","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ucn-017068f2-edab-4e9a-a182-954c455c57d1"],"keyword":["physiotherapy"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","noi":["Global-Local-2-4-4EAE1BDE8D1C6748DBD952838EEA44A2"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0218371","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ucn university college of northern denmark","l2":"DI-UC","l2n":"University Colleges","l3":"DI-UC-UCN","l3nl":"University College of Northern Denmark","l3nn":"UCN University College of Northern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"UCN University College of Northern Denmark","namevar":["Professionshøjskolen UCN, UCN Direktion, UCN Velfærd, Fysioterapeutuddannelsen"],"nora_code":"DI-UC-UCN","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Uffe","key":"laessoepau","last":"Laessoe","locid":"f2c779f7-db32-43f5-8856-c9708e0a90aa","name":"Læssøe, Uffe","name_org":"Laessoe, Uffe","orcid":"0000-0001-5388-1671","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Camilla Bille","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Camilla Bille","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Line Noerkjaer","key":"schunckpau","last":"Schunck","name":"Schunck, Line Noerkjaer","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Line Jensen","key":"lehmannpau","last":"Lehmann","name":"Lehmann, Line Jensen","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Halla","key":"iversenpau","last":"Iversen","name":"Iversen, Halla","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["31246971"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0218371","issn":["19326203"],"issue":"6","journal":"PLOS ONE","paperid":"e0218371","publisher":"Public Library of Science","status":"p","title":"PLOS ONE","volume":"14"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85068988822"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All University Colleges","DI-ALL","DI-UC","DI-UC-ALL","University College of Northern Denmark","University Colleges"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ucn-017068f2-edab-4e9a-a182-954c455c57d1"],"src":["UCN University College of Northern Denmark"],"title":"Age related differences in balance approached by a novel dual-task test of anticipatory postural control strategies","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-650036","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"PURPOSE. We describe changes in choroidal thickness from age 11 to 16 years and its association with ocular biometrics and body development. METHOD. In this longitudinal, population-based observational study, choroidal thickness was measured subfoveally and 1- and 3-mm temporal thereof using enhanced depth imaging spectral domain optical coherence tomography. Analyses were stratified by sex and adjusted for age and the time of day that the scan was performed. RESULTS. The study included 687 participants (304 boys). Median (interquartile range [IQR]) age was 11.5 (0.6) years at baseline and 16.6 (0.3) years at follow-up. Mean increase in choroidal thickness was 33, 27, and 11 lm at the three respective locations. The subfoveal choroid thickened less in eyes whose axial length increased more (boys, b ¼ 85 lm/mm; 95% confidence interval [CI], 104 to 66, P < 0.0001; girls, b ¼ 105 lm/mm; 95% CI, 121 to 89, P < 0.0001) and in eyes with a more negative refractive development (boys, 11 lm/diopters [D]; 95% CI, 4.0 to 18, P ¼ 0.0022; girls, 22 lm/D; 95% CI, 16 to 27, P < 0.0001). Subfoveal choroidal thickness increased less in girls who underwent early puberty (Tanner stage 4 vs. 1; 39 lm’ 95% CI, 72 to 5.9, P ¼ 0.021) and who had a longer baseline axial length (b ¼ 8.6 lm/mm; 95% CI, 15 to 2.7, P ¼ 0.0043), and more in girls who grew taller (b ¼ 0.9 lm/cm; 95% CI, 0.1 to 1.7, P ¼ 0.026). CONCLUSIONS. The choroid increased in thickness from age 11 to 16 years. The increase was greater in girls with later sexual maturation and smaller in eyes that added more axial length and had a relatively negative refractive development. Keywords: choroidal thickness changes, longitudinal study, cohort study, axial eye growth","author_count":7,"contributors":["Hansen, Mathias Hvidtfelt","Li, Xiao Qiang","Larsen, Michael","Olsen, Else Marie","Skovgaard, Anne Mette","Kessel, Line","Munch, Inger Christine"],"cuid":["S02-099.36-039819"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Hansen, Mathias Hvidtfelt"],"keyword":["Axial eye growth","Choroidal thickness changes","Cohort study","Longitudinal study"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"five year change in choroidal thickness in relation to body development and axial eye elongation the ccc000000002000 eye study","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1167/iovs.19-26807","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-099.36-039819","identifier":[{"id":"85072581182","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000488842600003","type":"wos"},{"id":"31546258","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-046780fd-718e-43fd-9c1d-73943bb52e34","sdu-5df5a801-638b-42c4-a924-87cf8ad49198"],"keyword":["axial eye growth","choroidal thickness changes","cohort study","longitudinal study"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-DCD98110A903DF9352951155AFD62B8E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/159958769/Open_Access_version","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1167/iovs.19-26807","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/228958062/Five_Year_Change_in_Choroidal_Thickness_in_Relation_to_Body_Development_and_Axial_Eye_Elongation.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, The Danish Stem Cell Center, Developmental biology - endoderm and pancreas development Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, National Institute of Public Health"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"dk","key":"region zealand","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-ZEA","l3nl":"Region Zealand","l3nn":"Region Zealand","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region Zealand","namevar":["Zealand University Hospital, Roskilde"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-ZEA","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Mathias Hvidtfelt","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","name":"Hansen, Mathias Hvidtfelt","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Xiao Qiang","key":"lipau","last":"Li","name":"Li, Xiao Qiang","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","first":"Michael","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"1a555c4a-f5eb-40fe-8f79-6442177ac676","name":"Larsen, Michael","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Else Marie","key":"olsenpau","last":"Olsen","locid":"9be69922-e68e-4556-9b40-34a82fc88a15","name":"Olsen, Else Marie","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Anne Mette","key":"skovgaardpau","last":"Skovgaard","locid":"16faee51-f101-47f9-8757-c611e52d7381","name":"Skovgaard, Anne Mette","orcid":"0000-0002-8793-8303","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Line","key":"kesselpau","last":"Kessel","locid":"3201472a-2f23-417e-bd01-a7950e2ff370","name":"Kessel, Line","orcid":"0000-0002-9375-1510","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 4","first":"Inger Christine","key":"munchpau","last":"Munch","locid":"a1af1ee5-c4b4-44d8-a7af-b7c1ce0882ac","name":"Munch, Inger Christine","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["31546258"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1167/iovs.19-26807","issn":["01460404","02730189"],"issn_org":["15525783"],"issue":"12","journal":"Investigative Ophthalmology & Visual Science","pages":"3930-3936","status":"p","title":"Investigative Ophthalmology & Visual Science","volume":"60"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85072581182"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Region Zealand","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-046780fd-718e-43fd-9c1d-73943bb52e34","sdu-5df5a801-638b-42c4-a924-87cf8ad49198"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Five-Year Change in Choroidal Thickness in Relation to Body Development and Axial Eye Elongation : The CCC2000 Eye Study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.36-039819","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Background/objectives: Individuals with high pre-treatment bacterial Prevotella-to-Bacteroides (P/B) ratio have been reported to lose more body weight on diets high in fiber than subjects with a low P/B ratio. Therefore, the aim of the present study was to examine potential differences in dietary weight loss responses between participants with low and high P/B. Subjects/methods: Eighty overweight participants were randomized (52 completed) to a 500 kcal/d energy deficit diet with a macronutrient composition of 30 energy percentage (E%) fat, 52 E% carbohydrate and 18 E% protein either high (â‰ˆ1500 mg calcium/day) or low ( â‰¤ 600 mg calcium/day) in dairy products for 24 weeks. Body weight, body fat, and dietary intake (by 7-day dietary records) were determined. Individuals were dichotomized according to their pre-treatment P/B ratio derived from 16S rRNA gene sequencing of collected fecal samples to test the potential modification of dietary effects using linear mixed models. Results: Independent of the randomized diets, individuals with high P/B lost 3.8 kg (95%CI, 1.8,5.8; P <0.001) more body weight and 3.8 kg (95% CI, 1.1, 6.5; P = 0.005) more body fat compared to individuals with low P/B. After adjustment for multiple covariates, individuals with high P/B ratio lost 8.3 kg (95% CI, 5.8;10.9, P <0.001) more body weight when consuming above compared to below 30 g fiber/10MJ whereas this weight loss was 3.2 kg (95% CI, 0.8;5.5, P = 0.008) among individuals with low P/B ratio [Mean difference: 5.1 kg (95% CI, 1.7;8.6, P = 0.003)]. Partial correlation coefficients between fiber intake and weight change was 0.90 (P <0.001) among individuals with high P/B ratio and 0.25 (P = 0.29) among individuals with low P/B ratio. Conclusions: Individuals with high P/B lost more body weight and body fat compared to individuals with low P/B, confirming that individuals with a high P/B are more susceptible to weight loss on a diet rich in fiber.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Hjorth, Mads F.","Blædel, Trine","Bendtsen, Line Q.","Lorenzen, Janne K.","Holm, Jacob B.","Kiilerich, Pia","Roager, Henrik Munch","Kristiansen, Karsten","Larsen, Lesli H.","Astrup, Arne"],"cuid":["S02-099.92-037100"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Asia, East"],"country":["China"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","BGI"],"keyword":["Faculty of Science","Obesity","Randomised controlled trials"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"i prevotella i to i bacteroides i ratio predicts body weight and fat loss success on 000000000024 week diets varying in macronutrient composition and dietary fiber results from a post hoc analysis","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/248805586/Prevotella_to_Bacteroides_ratio_predicts_body_weight_and_fat_loss_success_on_24_week_diets_varying_in_macronutrient_composition_and_dietary_fiber_results_from_a_post_hoc_analysis_version_of_record_.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Funding The study was supported by the Danish Council for Strategic Research and the Danish Dairy Research foundation. The present stratified analysis was supported by a grant from Gelesis Inc. Conflict of interest MFH and AA are co-inventers on a pending provisional patent application on the use of biomarkers for prediction of weight loss responses. The work reported in this manuscript was funded by grants from Gelesis Inc. AA is a consultant for Gelesis Inc. concerning scientific advice unrelated to the current paper. The remaining authors declare that they have no conflict of interest."],