-9dd3-59630ea8c815","name":"Jølving, Line Riis","name_org":"Jolving, L. R.","orcid":"0000-0002-7433-7656","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"J.","key":"fedderpau","last":"Fedder","locid":"51b716fd-ee92-46ef-8d70-04bf8995c772","name":"Fedder, Jens","name_org":"Fedder, J.","orcid":"0000-0001-5383-5501","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"B. M.","key":"norgardpau","last":"Norgard","locid":"3d41d6fb-8f1c-4899-9ebd-b72f88e3935d","name":"Nørgård, Bente Mertz","name_org":"Norgard, B. M.","orcid":"0000-0002-9514-3033","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["02681161","14602350"],"issn_org":["14602350"],"issue":"Suppl. 1","journal":"Human Reproduction","pages":"79-80","publisher":"Oxford University Press","status":"p","title":"Human Reproduction","volume":"34"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-15f77fd2-f4bb-4185-b978-306381b76df3"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"No decreased chance of a live born child in women with epilepsy after assisted reproduction treatment: a nationwide cohort study","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-579503","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Background: Good postural control may require minimal attention in normal daily living situations. A well learned sensory-motor control is considered to be automatic and will allow the individual residual attentional capacity for other concurrent tasks. The assessment of a patient’s postural control may be of relevance in the clinic. A given test on motor control may be misguiding, however, as the participant may use his/her residual attentional capacity to perform well in the test. Therefore, emphasis has been made on the importance of assessing postural control using dual-task tests. These tests may combine a motor task and a cognitive task. Different attentional requirements may exist, depending on the task, the environment and the balance abilities of the individual. In a clinical setting it remains a challenge to control the individual’s prioritization between the tasks and it is difficult to monitor the performance in a secondary task. To overcome these challenges, we evaluate a new approach to dual-task testing. Purpose: Validation of a new version of a dual-task test. Methods: Fifty-four community-dwelling elderly (age 65+) and a reference group of 20 young participants performed a test in which they could utilize cues to improve their base line performance. In the construct validation of the test, it was assumed that the postural control was poorer in the elderly group. In most dual task tests the outcome measure is the decrement in primary task performance with a concurrent secondary task. In this new test the participants may improve the performance with the secondary task. The primary task requires 15 locomotion to touch three lights placed in different zones. In the secondary task, specific colour codes may guide the participant’s anticipatory postural control strategies toward these zones. A better performance time can be obtained, provided the level of postural control of the individual is leaving residual attention capacity for this cognitive task and allows the individual to use these cues. Results: The young group improved their performance time by 23.9% (10.7) and 7.1% (14.2) with a cue and a reverse cue respectively. The elderly did not improve their performance time and the difference between groups was highly significant. The test did not distinguish between the individuals within the elderly group with respect to fall history. This was ascribed to a floor effect. Conclusion: The test reveals the capacity to use cues for anticipatory postural control strategies in a dual task setting and thereby estimates the automatization of the postural control. With respect to differentiation between the levels of postural control within the elderly group, the dual task aspects of the test add no value. Implications: With a further technical development of the setup, the test may be useful in a clinical setting where a dual-task testing approach is required. Keywords: Postural control, dual task,","author_count":5,"contributors":["Læssøe, Uffe","Larsen, Camilla B","Schunck, Line N","Lehmann, Line J","Iversen, Halla"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-651556"],"event":{"end":"2019-05-13","place":"Geneve, ch","start":"2019-05-10","title":"World Confederation for Physical Therapy 2019","year":"2019"},"filters":{"added":"2023-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Physiotherapy"],"level":"sci","oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-UC"],"level3":{"UC":["DI-UC-ALL","DI-UC-UCN"]},"level3all":["DI-UC-UCN"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a different approach to dual task testing","src":["ucn"],"src-code":["DI-UC-UCN"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.abstractstosubmit.com/wcpt2019/archive/#/viewer/abstract/2771","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-651556","ids":["ucn-8e252d65-9857-4c42-80b4-33f8ccdefa14"],"keyword":["physiotherapy"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","noi":["Local-1-1-52306A5682523E94C57F948A2D560455"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.abstractstosubmit.com/wcpt2019/archive/#/viewer/abstract/2771","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ucn university college of northern denmark","l2":"DI-UC","l2n":"University Colleges","l3":"DI-UC-UCN","l3nl":"University College of Northern Denmark","l3nn":"UCN University College of Northern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"UCN University College of Northern Denmark","namevar":["Professionshøjskolen UCN, UCN Direktion, UCN Velfærd, Fysioterapeutuddannelsen"],"nora_code":"DI-UC-UCN","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Uffe","key":"laessoepau","last":"Laessoe","locid":"f2c779f7-db32-43f5-8856-c9708e0a90aa","name":"Læssøe, Uffe","name_org":"Laessoe, Uffe","orcid":"0000-0001-5388-1671","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Camilla B","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Camilla B","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Line N","key":"schunckpau","last":"Schunck","name":"Schunck, Line N","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Line J","key":"lehmannpau","last":"Lehmann","name":"Lehmann, Line J","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Halla","key":"iversenpau","last":"Iversen","name":"Iversen, Halla","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All University Colleges","DI-ALL","DI-UC","DI-UC-ALL","University College of Northern Denmark","University Colleges"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ucn-8e252d65-9857-4c42-80b4-33f8ccdefa14"],"src":["UCN University College of Northern Denmark"],"title":"A Different Approach to Dual Task Testing","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-651556","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Purpose: Multi-morbidity and polypharmacy are common among older people. It is essential to provide a better understanding of the complexity of prescription drug use among older adults to optimise rational pharmacotherapy. Population-based utilisation data in this age group is limited. Using the Danish nationwide health registries, we aimed to characterise drug use among Danish individuals ≥ 60 years. Methods: This is a descriptive population-based study assessing drug prescription patterns in 2015 in the full Danish population aged ≥ 60 years. The use of specific therapeutic subgroups and chemical subgroups and its dependence on age were described using descriptive statistics. Profiles of drug combination patterns were evaluated using latent class analysis. Results: We included 1,424,775 residents (median age 70 years, 53% women). Of all the older adults, 89% filled at least one prescription during 2015. The median number of drug groups used was five per person. The most used single drug groups were paracetamol and analogues (34%), statins (33%) and platelet aggregation inhibitors (24%). Eighteen drug profiles with different drug combination patterns were identified. One drug profile with expected use of zero drugs and 11 drug profiles expected to receive more than five different therapeutic subgroup drugs were identified. Conclusion: The use of drugs is extensive both at the population level and increasing with age at an individual level. Separating the population into different homogenous groups related to drug use resulted in 18 different drug profiles, of which 11 drug profiles received on average more than five different therapeutic subgroup drugs.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Christensen, Line Due","Reilev, Mette","Juul-Larsen, Helle Gybel","Jørgensen, Lillian Mørch","Kaae, Susanne","Andersen, Ove","Pottegård, Anton","Petersen, Janne"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-044930"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Christensen, Line J.L."],"keyword":["Drug profiles","Drug utilisation","Older adults","Pharmacoepidemiology","Prescription drugs"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"use of prescription drugs in the older adult population a nationwide pharmacoepidemiological study","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/249496608/Use_of_prescription_drugs_in_the_older_adult_population_a_nationwide_pharmacoepidemiological_study_accepted_version_.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-044930","identifier":[{"id":"30949726","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85064515366","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-a2553b70-9560-4e1a-bd6d-0eb1467c4f47","sdu-02c7e786-099a-490c-a3ac-61c61d87e07e"],"keyword":["Drug profiles","Drug utilisation","Older adults","Pharmacoepidemiology","Prescription drugs"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-46FCA6C5EDAB7F7046C92F68C6AF4E84"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/155303966/Use_of_prescription_drugs_in_the_older_adult_population.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/249496608/Use_of_prescription_drugs_in_the_older_adult_population_a_nationwide_pharmacoepidemiological_study_accepted_version_.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southe