{"abstract":"Chemokine receptors play important roles in the immune system and are linked to several human diseases. The initial contact of chemokines with their receptors depends on highly specified extracellular receptor features. Here we investigate the importance of conserved extracellular disulfide bridges and aromatic residues in extracellular loop 2 (ECL2) for ligand binding and activation in the chemokine receptor CCR8. We used IP3 accumulation and radioligand binding experiments to determine the impact of receptor mutagenesis on both chemokine and small molecule agonist and antagonist binding and action in CCR8. We find that the 7 transmembrane (7TM) receptor conserved disulfide bridge (7TM bridge) linking transmembrane helix (TM)III and ECL2 is crucial for chemokine and small molecule action, whereas the chemokine receptor conserved disulfide bridge between the N terminus and TMVII is needed only for chemokines. Furthermore, we find that two distinct aromatic residues in ECL2, Y184 (Cys+1) and Y187 (Cys+4), are crucial for binding of the CC chemokines CCL1 (agonist) and MC148 (antagonist), respectively, but not for small molecule binding. Finally, using in silico modeling, we predict an aromatic cluster of interaction partners for Y187 in TMIV (F171) and TMV (W194). We show in vitro that these residues are crucial for the binding and action of MC148, thus supporting their participation in an aromatic cluster with Y187. This aromatic cluster appears to be present in a large number of CC chemokine receptors and thereby could play a more general role to be exploited in future drug development targeting these receptors.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Barington, Line","Rummel, Pia Cwarzko","Lückmann, Michael","Pihl, Heidi","Larsen, Olav","Daugvilaite, Viktorija","Johnsen, Anders H.","Frimurer, Thomas M.","Thiele, Stefanie","Rosenkilde, Mette M."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-502638"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"role of conserved disulfide bridges and aromatic residues in extracellular loop 000000000002 of chemokine receptor ccr000000000008 for chemokine and small molecule binding","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-502638","identifier":[{"id":"84979755882","type":"scopus"},{"id":"27226537","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC4965569","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-b6e005cf-1f84-4ba2-b98d-a3d09a0d9e25"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D8B96D560FFF86CD1C8D4E0494DD0C49"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Neuroscience, Molpharm Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Line","key":"baringtonpau","last":"Barington","locid":"46c79903-175f-47b0-9539-6ff5c28bff0b","name":"Barington, Line","orcid":"0000-0003-4282-6970","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Pia C","key":"rummelpau","last":"Rummel","locid":"ef715828-41db-4ce0-9d91-943b16277369","name":"Rummel, Pia Cwarzko","name_org":"Rummel, Pia C","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Michael","key":"lückmannpau","last":"Lückmann","locid":"7458ae2b-39e7-4104-96b9-faa17fa5c548","name":"Lückmann, Michael","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Heidi","key":"pihlpau","last":"Pihl","name":"Pihl, Heidi","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Olav","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"321e5538-1221-49f8-a37b-68e2f9410c83","name":"Larsen, Olav","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Viktorija","key":"daugvilaitepau","last":"Daugvilaite","locid":"54d160ba-5c25-4e4f-9560-377718bb0ac5","name":"Daugvilaite, Viktorija","orcid":"0000-0001-8786-348X","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Anders H.","key":"johnsenpau","last":"Johnsen","name":"Johnsen, Anders H.","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Thomas M","key":"frimurerpau","last":"Frimurer","locid":"4592246e-6be7-4da6-bbc4-fa9f5061d96b","name":"Frimurer, Thomas M.","name_org":"Frimurer, Thomas M","orcid":"0000-0002-9682-8245","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Stefanie","key":"karlshøjpau","last":"Karlshøj","locid":"97bcf8ec-1787-4c2d-a12c-3556784e9e29","name":"Thiele, Stefanie","name_org":"Karlshøj, Stefanie","orcid":"0000-0001-6505-0883","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Mette M","key":"rosenkildepau","last":"Rosenkilde","locid":"d902feb8-1594-4fa8-bcb1-9f646dc30dd3","name":"Rosenkilde, Mette M.","name_org":"Rosenkilde, Mette M","orcid":"0000-0001-9600-3254","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["27226537"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1074/jbc.m115.706747","issn":["00219258","10678816"],"issn_org":["00219258"],"issue":"31","journal":"Journal of Biological Chemistry","pages":"16208-16220","status":"p","title":"Journal of Biological Chemistry","title_org":"The Journal of Biological Chemistry","volume":"291"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84979755882"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-b6e005cf-1f84-4ba2-b98d-a3d09a0d9e25"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Role of Conserved Disulfide Bridges and Aromatic Residues in Extracellular Loop 2 of Chemokine Receptor CCR8 for Chemokine and Small Molecule Binding","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-502638","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Neutrophils expressing formyl peptide receptor 2 (FPR2) play key roles in host defense, immune regulation, and resolution of inflammation. Consequently, the search for FPR2-specific modulators has attracted much attention due to its therapeutic potential. Earlier described agonists for this receptor display potent activity for the human receptor (FPR2) but low activity for the mouse receptor orthologue (Fpr2), rendering them inapplicable in murine models of human disease. Here we describe a novel FPR2 agonist, the proteolytically stable α-peptide/β-peptoid hybrid Lau-((S)-Aoc)-(Lys-βNphe)6-NH2 (F2M2), showing comparable potency in activating human and mouse neutrophils by inducing a rise in intracellular Ca2+ concentration and assembly of the superoxide-generating NADPH oxidase. This FPR2/Fpr2 agonist contains a headgroup consisting of a 2-aminooctanoic acid (Aoc) residue acylated with lauric acid (C12 fatty acid), which is linked to a peptide/peptoid repeat ((Lys-βNphe)6-NH2). Both the fatty acid moiety and the (S)-Aoc residue were required for FPR2/Fpr2 activation. This type of proteolytically stable FPR2-specific peptidomimetics may serve as valuable tools for future analysis of FPR2 signaling as well as for development of prophylactic immunomodulatory therapy. This novel class of cross-species FPR2/Fpr2 agonists should enable translation of results obtained with mouse neutrophils (and disease models) into enhanced understanding of human inflammatory and immune diseases.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Holdfeldt, Andre","Skovbakke, Sarah Line","Winther, Malene","Gabl, Michael","Nielsen, Christina","Perez-Gassol, Iris","Larsen, Camilla Josephine","Wang, Ji Ming","Karlsson, Anna","Dahlgren, Claes","Forsman, Huamei","Franzyk, Henrik"],"cuid":["S02-096.62-041523"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","National Institutes of Health","Sahlgrenska University Hospital"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the lipidated peptidomimetic lau s aoc lys βnphe 000000000006 nh000000000002 is a novel formyl peptide receptor 000000000002 agonist that activates both human and mouse neutrophil nadph oxidase","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1074/jbc.m116.736850","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-096.62-041523","identifier":[{"id":"27422818","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84987899369","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC5025677","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-38359ec0-1236-4877-a044-cab3fd6abdf7","ku-5b0e81ee-6f7a-4474-ab79-f44e56cf7cc1"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-65250B87C610B43719607EB69B0AEC5C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1074/jbc.m116.736850","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/329687774/Fulltext.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"se","key":"sahlgrenska university hospital","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Sahlgrenska University Hospital","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Drug Design and Pharmacology, Natural Products and Peptides"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"national institutes of health","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"National Institutes of Health","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Andre","key":"holdfeldtpau","last":"Holdfeldt","name":"Holdfeldt, Andre","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Sarah Line","key":"skovbakkepau","last":"Skovbakke","locid":"6c43699f-79b1-42e1-bc75-5783c8024f41","name":"Skovbakke, Sarah Line","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Malene","key":"wintherpau","last":"Winther","name":"Winther, Malene","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Michael","key":"gablpau","last":"Gabl","name":"Gabl, Michael","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Christina","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Christina","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Iris","key":"perezgassolpau","last":"Perez-Gassol","name":"Perez-Gassol, Iris","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Camilla Josephine","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Camilla Josephine","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Ji Ming","key":"wangpau","last":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ji Ming","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Anna","key":"karlssonpau","last":"Karlsson","name":"Karlsson, Anna","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Claes","key":"dahlgrenpau","last":"Dahlgren","name":"Dahlgren, Claes","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Huamei","key":"forsmanpau","last":"Forsman","name":"Forsman, Huamei","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Henrik","key":"franzykpau","last":"Franzyk","locid":"5120ce68-6886-4ac8-8178-310a47d19261","name":"Franzyk, Henrik","orcid":"0000-0002-2822-1927","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["27422818"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1074/jbc.m116.736850","issn":["00219258","10678816"],"issn_org":["00219258"],"issue":"38","journal":"Journal of Biological Chemistry","pages":"19888-19899","status":"p","title":"Journal of Biological Chemistry","volume":"291"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84987899369"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-38359ec0-1236-4877-a044-cab3fd6abdf7","ku-5b0e81ee-6f7a-4474-ab79-f44e56cf7cc1"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Lipidated Peptidomimetic Lau-((S)-Aoc)-(Lys-βNphe)6-NH2Is a Novel Formyl Peptide Receptor 2 Agonist That Activates Both Human and Mouse Neutrophil NADPH Oxidase","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-096.62-041523","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"In recent years, several genes have been causally associated with epilepsy. However, making a genetic diagnosis in a patient can still be difficult, since extensive phenotypic and genetic heterogeneity has been observed in many monogenic epilepsies. This study aimed to analyze the genetic basis of a wide spectrum of epilepsies with age of onset spanning from the neonatal period to adulthood. A gene panel targeting 46 epilepsy genes was used on a cohort of 216 patients consecutively referred for panel testing. The patients had a range of different epilepsies from benign neonatal seizures to epileptic encephalopathies (EEs). Potentially causative variants were evaluated by literature and database searches, submitted to bioinformatic prediction algorithms, and validated by Sanger sequencing. If possible, parents were included for segregation analysis. We identified a presumed disease-causing variant in 49 (23%) of the 216 patients. The variants were found in 19 different genes including SCN1A, STXBP1, CDKL5, SCN2A, SCN8A, GABRA1, KCNA2, and STX1B. Patients with neonatal-onset epilepsies had the highest rate of positive findings (57%). The overall yield for patients with EEs was 32%, compared to 17% among patients with generalized epilepsies and 16% in patients with focal or multifocal epilepsies. By the use of a gene panel consisting of 46 epilepsy genes, we were able to find a disease-causing genetic variation in 23% of the analyzed patients. The highest yield was found among patients with neonatal-onset epilepsies and EEs.","author_count":37,"contributors":["Møller, Rikke S","Larsen, Line H G","Johannesen, Katrine M","Talvik, Inga","Talvik, Tiina","Vaher, Ulvi","Miranda, Maria J","Farooq, Muhammad","Nielsen, Jens E K","Svendsen, Lene Lavard","Kjelgaard, Ditte B","Linnet, Karen Markussen","Hao, Qin","Uldall, Peter","Frangu, Mimoza","Tommerup, Niels","Baig, Shahid M","Abdullah, Uzma","Born, Alfred P","Gellert, Pia","Nikanorova, Marina","Olofsson, Kern","Jepsen, Birgit","Marjanovic, Dragan","Al-Zehhawi, Lana I K","Peñalva, Sofia J","Krag-Olsen, Bente","Brusgaard, Klaus","Hjalgrim, Helle","Rubboli, Guido","Pal, Deb K","Dahl, Hans A"],"cuid":["S03-100.00-028709"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["America, South","Asia, South","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Argentina","Estonia","Pakistan","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","CEMIC: Center for Medical Education and Clinical Research","King's College London","National Institute for Biotechnology and Genetic Engineering (NIBGE)-PIEAS","Tallinn Children’s Hospital","Tartu University Clinic","University of Tartu"],"keyword":["Epileptic encephalopathies","Familial epilepsies","Seizures"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-FILA","DI-HO-ZEA"],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-FILA","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"gene panel testing in epileptic encephalopathies and familial epilepsies","src":["au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5073625/pdf/msy-0007-0210.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S03-100.00-028709","identifier":[{"id":"PMC5073625","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"27781031","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84983748486","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-dd314d09-2b25-429d-af98-529df3543826","ku-c6f6d84c-b9df-437a-a5cf-d5858a6536f3","sdu-0316c54c-130b-46cf-ae1b-7d1cee865485"],"keyword":["Epileptic encephalopathies","Familial epilepsies","Gene panel testing","Seizures"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientif