k Teknologivurdering af Robotassisteret kirurgi","type":"Report","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-080.77-025726","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
{"abstract":"Maari is a spontaneously fermented food condiment made from baobab tree seeds in West African countries. This type of product is considered to be safe, being consumed by millions of people on a daily basis. However, due to the spontaneous nature of the fermentation the human pathogen Bacillus cereus occasionally occurs in Maari. This study characterizes succession patterns and pathogenic potential of B. cereus isolated from the raw materials (ash, water from a drilled well (DW) and potash), seed mash throughout fermentation (0-96h), after steam cooking and sun drying (final product) from two production sites of Maari. Aerobic mesophilic bacterial (AMB) counts in raw materials were of 10(5)cfu/ml in DW, and ranged between 6.5×10(3) and 1.2×10(4)cfu/g in potash, 10(9)-10(10)cfu/g in seed mash during fermentation and 10(7) - 10(9) after sun drying. Fifty three out of total 290 AMB isolates were identified as B. cereus sensu lato by use of ITS-PCR and grouped into 3 groups using PCR fingerprinting based on Escherichia coli phage-M13 primer (M13-PCR). As determined by panC gene sequencing, the isolates of B. cereus belonged to PanC types III and IV with potential for high cytotoxicity. Phylogenetic analysis of concatenated sequences of glpF, gmk, ilvD, pta, pur, pycA and tpi revealed that the M13-PCR group 1 isolates were related to B. cereus biovar anthracis CI, while the M13-PCR group 2 isolates were identical to cereulide (emetic toxin) producing B. cereus strains. The M13-PCR group 1 isolates harboured poly-γ-D-glutamic acid capsule biosynthesis genes capA, capB and capC showing 99-100% identity with the environmental B. cereus isolate 03BB108. Presence of cesB of the cereulide synthetase gene cluster was confirmed by PCR in M13-PCR group 2 isolates. The B. cereus harbouring the cap genes were found in potash, DW, cooking water and at 8h fermentation. The \"emetic\" type B. cereus were present in DW, the seed mash at 48-72h of fermentation and in the final product, while the remaining isolates (PanC type IV) were detected in ash, at 48-72h fermentation and in the final product. This work sheds light on the succession and pathogenic potential of B. cereus species in traditional West African food condiment and clarifies their phylogenetic relatedness to B. cereus biovar anthracis. Future implementation of GMP and HACCP and development of starter cultures for controlled Maari fermentations will help to ensure a safe product.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Thorsen, Line","Kando, Christine Kere","Sawadogo, Hagrétou","Larsen, Nadja","Diawara, Bréhima","Ouédraogo, Georges Anicet","Hendriksen, Niels Bohse","Jespersen, Lene"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-016881"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Africa"],"corresponding_author":["Jespersen, Lene"],"country":["Burkina Faso"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Food Technology Department (DTA/IRSAT/CNRST)","Rural Development Institute, Polytechnic University of Bobo-Dioulasso"],"keyword":["Bacillus cereus","Bacillus cereus biovar anthracis","Food fermentation","Maari"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"characteristics and phylogeny of i bacillus cereus i strains isolated from maari a traditional west african food condiment","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijfoodmicro.2014.11.026","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-016881","identifier":[{"id":"25528535","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84919458998","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-39b5659d-3455-4a4e-8326-29d6f5c7d03a","ku-715a2251-f762-4b82-b9b1-3c14429a9ceb"],"keyword":["Bacillus cereus","Bacillus cereus biovar anthracis","Food fermentation","Maari"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-91C1729279D100A5A52A07642F2C742C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://www.scopus.com/inward/record.url?scp=84919458998&partnerID=8YFLogxK","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijfoodmicro.2014.11.026","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2015","yearSub":"2015"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Food Science, Food Microbiology, Gut Health, and Fermentation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Unspecified organisation"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"bf","key":"food technology department","loc":["Burkina Faso","Africa"],"name":"Food Technology Department (DTA/IRSAT/CNRST)","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"bf","key":"rural development institute polytechnic university of bobo dioulasso","loc":["Burkina Faso","Africa"],"name":"Rural Development Institute, Polytechnic University of Bobo-Dioulasso","used":1},{"affno":"5","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, Department of Environmental Science, Department of Environmental Science - Environmental Microbiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Line","key":"thorsenpau","last":"Thorsen","locid":"c882a60f-763a-4496-93be-335fe3451a41","name":"Thorsen, Line","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Christine Kere","key":"kandopau","last":"Kando","name":"Kando, Christine Kere","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Hagrétou","key":"sawadogopau","last":"Sawadogo","name":"Sawadogo, Hagrétou","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Nadja","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"be39c304-b5b1-4ff3-bef0-a00768b752d3","name":"Larsen, Nadja","orcid":"0000-0002-5899-4883","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Bréhima","key":"diawarapau","last":"Diawara","name":"Diawara, Bréhima","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Georges Anicet","key":"ouédraogopau","last":"Ouédraogo","name":"Ouédraogo, Georges Anicet","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Niels Bohse","key":"hendriksenpau","last":"Hendriksen","locid":"50fd9962-24ef-4560-9838-1bb6da39e607","name":"Hendriksen, Niels Bohse","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lene","key":"jespersenpau","last":"Jespersen","locid":"a156c6e3-9562-4d35-9305-53a39c62e076","name":"Jespersen, Lene","orcid":"0000-0002-2555-5948","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["25528535"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.ijfoodmicro.2014.11.026","issn":["01681605","18793460"],"issn_org":["01681605"],"journal":"International Journal of Food Microbiology","pages":"70-78","publisher":"Elsevier BV","publisher_org":"Elsevier B.V.","status":"p","title":"International Journal of Food Microbiology","volume":"196"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84919458998"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-39b5659d-3455-4a4e-8326-29d6f5c7d03a","ku-715a2251-f762-4b82-b9b1-3c14429a9ceb"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Characteristics and phylogeny of Bacillus cereus strains isolated from Maari, a traditional West African food condiment","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-016881","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
{"abstract":"OBJECTIVES: To estimate the prevalence of Danish RA patients currently on biologic monotherapy and compare the effectiveness and drug adherence of biologic therapies applied as monotherapy. METHODS: All RA patients registered in the Danish biologics database (DANBIO) as receiving biologic DMARD (bDMARD) treatment as monotherapy without concomitant conventional synthetic DMARDs (csDMARDs) during the study period 1 May, 2011 through 30 April 2013 were eligible for inclusion. All patient files were checked to ensure that they were in accordance with the treatment registration in DANBIO. Descriptive statistics for prevalence, effectiveness and drug adherence of bDMARD monotherapy were calculated. RESULTS: Of the 775 patients on bDMARD monotherapy, adalimumab (21.3%), etanercept (36.6%) and tocilizumab (15.3%) were the most prevalent biologic agents administered. At the 6-month follow-up, the overall crude clinical disease activity index remission rate in patients still on a biologic drug was 22%, the 28-joint DAS remission rate was 41% and the response rate of those with a 50% improvement in ACR criteria was 28%. At the 6-month follow-up, the drug adherence rates were similar for the different bDMARDs, with the exception of infliximab, which had significantly poorer drug adherence (P < 0.001). The overall drug adherence (except for infliximab) was approximately 70% after 2 years. CONCLUSION: Nearly one in five (19%) biologic treatments for RA was prescribed in Denmark as monotherapy, of which 70% were on monotherapy from bio-initiation and 30% were on monotherapy after cessation of a concomitant csDMARD. Acceptable drug adherence and remission rates were achieved with bDMARDs. With the exception of infliximab, no statistically significant differences were observed between anti-TNFs and biologics with other modes of action.","author_count":43,"contributors":["Jørgensen, Tanja Schjødt","Kristensen, Lars Erik","Christensen, Robin","Bliddal, Henning","Lorenzen,