arsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"Bacillus spp. are commonly used as probiotic species in the feed industry, however, their benefits need to be confirmed. This study describes a high throughput screening combined with the detailed characterization of endospore-forming bacteria with the aim to identify new Bacillus spp. strains for use as probiotic additives in pig feed. A total of 245 bacterial isolates derived from African fermented food, feces and soil were identified by 16S rRNA gene sequencing and screened for antimicrobial activity and growth in the presence of antibiotics, bile salts and at pH 4.0. Thirty-three Bacillus spp. isolates with the best characteristics were identified by gyrB and rpoB gene sequencing as B. amyloliquefaciens subsp. plantarum, B. amyloliquefaciens subsp. amyloliquefaciens, B. subtilis subsp. subtilis, B. licheniformis, B. mojavensis, B. pumilus and B. megaterium. These isolates were further investigated for their activity against the pathogenic bacteria, antibiotic susceptibility, sporulation rates, biofilm formation and production of glycosyl hydrolytic enzymes. Additionally, ten selected isolates were assessed for heat resistance of spores and the effect on porcine epithelial cells IPEC-J2. Isolates of B. amyloliquefaciens, B. subtilis and B. mojavensis, showed the best overall characteristics and, therefore, potential for usage as probiotic additives in feed. A large number of taxonomically diverse strains made it possible to reveal species and subspecies-specific trends, contributing to our understanding of the probiotic potential of Bacillus species.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Larsen, Nadja","Thorsen, Line","Kpikpi, Elmer Nayra","Stuer-Lauridsen, Birgitte","Cantor, Mette Dines","Nielsen, Bea","Brockmann, Elke","Derkx, Patrick M.F.","Jespersen, Lene"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-538528"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Africa"],"country":["Ghana"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Kwame Nkrumah University of Science and Techonlogy"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-NOSI"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-NOSI","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"characterization of em bacillus em spp strains for use as probiotic additives in pig feed","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-013-5343-6","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-538528","identifier":[{"id":"24201893","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84898920747","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-f321ac96-0ad7-462e-9b25-e1004c073f08"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-8D3A60D6C639FCF297B96752DF6C9333"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-013-5343-6","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Food Science, Food Microbiology, Gut Health, and Fermentation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gh","key":"kwame nkrumah university of science and techonlogy","loc":["Ghana","Africa"],"name":"Kwame Nkrumah University of Science and Techonlogy","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"novonesis","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-NOSI","l3nn":"Novonesis","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Novonesis","namevar":["Chr. Hansen"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-NOSI","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Nadja","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"be39c304-b5b1-4ff3-bef0-a00768b752d3","name":"Larsen, Nadja","orcid":"0000-0002-5899-4883","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Line","key":"thorsenpau","last":"Thorsen","locid":"c882a60f-763a-4496-93be-335fe3451a41","name":"Thorsen, Line","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Elmer Nayra","key":"kpikpipau","last":"Kpikpi","name":"Kpikpi, Elmer Nayra","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Birgitte","key":"stuerlauridsenpau","last":"Stuer-Lauridsen","name":"Stuer-Lauridsen, Birgitte","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Mette Dines","key":"cantorpau","last":"Cantor","name":"Cantor, Mette Dines","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Bea","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Bea","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Elke","key":"brockmannpau","last":"Brockmann","name":"Brockmann, Elke","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Patrick M.F.","key":"derkxpau","last":"Derkx","name":"Derkx, Patrick M.F.","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lene","key":"jespersenpau","last":"Jespersen","locid":"a156c6e3-9562-4d35-9305-53a39c62e076","name":"Jespersen, Lene","orcid":"0000-0002-2555-5948","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["24201893"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00253-013-5343-6","issn":["01757598","14320614"],"issn_org":["01757598"],"issue":"3","journal":"Applied Microbiology and Biotechnology","pages":"1105-1118","status":"p","title":"Applied Microbiology and Biotechnology","volume":"98"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84898920747"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Private Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-f321ac96-0ad7-462e-9b25-e1004c073f08"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Characterization of <em>Bacillus</em> spp. strains for use as probiotic additives in pig feed","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-538528","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"Objectives To describe the development of acronym use across five major medical specialties and to evaluate the technical and aesthetic quality of the acronyms. Design Acronyms obtained through a literature search of Pubmed.gov followed by a standardised assessment of acronym quality (BEAUTY and CHEATING criteria). Participants Randomised controlled trials within psychiatry, rheumatology, pulmonary medicine, endocrinology, and cardiology published between 2000 and 2012. Main outcome measures Prevalence proportion of acronyms and composite quality score for acronyms over time. Results 14 965 publications were identified, of which 18.3% (n=2737) contained an acronym in the title. Acronym use was more common among cardiological studies than among the other four medical specialties (40% v 8-15% in 2012, P<0.001). Except for within cardiology, the prevalence of acronyms increased over time, with the average prevalence proportion among the remaining four specialties increasing from 4.0% to 12.4% from 2000 to 2012 (P<0.001). The median combined acronym quality score decreased significantly over the study period (P<0.001), from a median 9.25 in 2000 to 5.50 in 2012. Conclusion From 2000 to 2012 the prevalence of acronyms in trial reports increased, coinciding with a substantial decrease in the technical and aesthetic quality of the acronyms. Strict enforcement of current guidelines on acronym construction by journal editors is necessary to ensure the proper use of acronyms in the future.","author_count":17,"contributors":["Pottegård, Anton","Haastrup, Maija Bruun","Stage, Tore Bjerregaard","Hansen, Morten Rix","Larsen, Kasper Søltoft","Meegaard, P. M.","Meegaard, L. H.","Horneberg, H.","Gils, Charlotte","Dideriksen, Dorthe","Aagaard, Lise","Almarsdóttir, Anna Birna","Hallas, Jesper","Damkier, Per"],"cuid":["S02-087.87-666776"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"search for humouristic and extravagant acronyms and thoroughly inappropriate names for important clinical trials scientific qualitative and quantitative systematic study","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.g7092","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-087.87-666776","identifier":[{"id":"25516539","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84919904545","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-0eadb702-b91a-4362-84c4-135ae3f41679","sdu-b3823190-50b4-401f-8563-6f070b8279da"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-823DDC5E09CAED4AD6EE0D288FF50183"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.g7092","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/229320026/bmj.g7092.full.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Public Health, Clinical Pharmacology and Pharmacy"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Facult