{"abstract":"Sleep pattern and circadian rhythms are regulated via the retinohypothalamic tract in response to stimulation of a subset of retinal ganglion cells, predominantly by blue light (450-490 nm). With age, the transmission of blue light to the retina is reduced because of the aging process of the human lens, and this may impair the photoentrainment of circadian rhythm leading to sleep disorders. The aim of the study was to examine the association between lens aging and sleep disorders.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Kessel, Line","Siganos, Galatios","Jørgensen, Torben","Larsen, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-437308"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"sleep disturbances are related to decreased transmission of blue light to the retina caused by lens yellowing","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5665/SLEEP.1242","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-437308","identifier":[{"id":"PMC3157663","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"21886359","type":"pmid"},{"id":"80052640754","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-497c2345-e17b-4ab3-a79c-47f6ab8514d7"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-E10D9ECB9173106D3D358CD41AAABF3D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5665/SLEEP.1242","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sc