09607692","14690705"],"issn_org":["09607692"],"issue":"3","journal":"Ultrasound in Obstetrics & Gynecology","pages":"281-287","publisher":"Wiley","status":"p","title":"Ultrasound in Obstetrics & Gynecology","title_org":"Ultrasound in obstetrics & gynecology : the official journal of the International Society of Ultrasound in Obstetrics and Gynecology","volume":"38"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-80052290013"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-27e5e790-1a22-4bde-bcd0-644383aa8af1","ku-6b2cf80f-fd1f-4e4e-bf9b-073a2adb4996","ku-a2e5542f-aa14-4d8e-86fd-4b57250f62e1","sdu-bd496fe8-426b-4234-b2db-9b77135cdbee"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Vaginal micronized progesterone and risk of preterm delivery in high-risk twin pregnancies : secondary analysis of a placebo-controlled randomized trial and meta-analysis","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S04-086.79-015504","year":"2011","yearsub":"2011"}
{"abstract":"AIMS: To assess cumulative glycemia, microvascular characteristics, and associated risk factors for diabetes in subjects with impaired glucose regulation. METHODS: Cross-sectional, population-based study comprising systemic characteristics in 6487 participants and ocular characteristics in 970 participants. RESULTS: Lens fluorescence, a quantitative index of life-long cumulative glycemia, was increased by 7.5% (CI(95) 0.37-15.1%) in subjects with impaired fasting glucose, by 13.0% (CI(95) 5.5-21%) in subjects with combined impaired fasting glucose and impaired glucose tolerance (IFG+IGT), and by 11.8% (CI(95) 6.8-17.1%) in subjects with screen-detected diabetes compared to normoglycemic subjects, adjusted for age, sex, and smoking. The prevalences of microalbuminuria and retinopathy were significantly increased in subjects with screen-detected diabetes after adjusting for age, sex and systolic blood pressure. The prevalences of associated risk factors for diabetes were elevated in all categories of abnormal glucose regulation compared to normoglycemic subjects. CONCLUSIONS: Life-long cumulative glycemia, microangiopathy, and associated risk factors for diabetes were significantly elevated in subjects with abnormal glucose metabolism, most prominently in subjects with IFG+IGT and in subjects with screen-detected diabetes. These results provide the first objective evidence that cumulative glycemic load is increased at the earliest stage of impaired glucose regulation.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Munch, Inger Christine","Larsen, Michael","Kessel, Line","Borch-Johnsen, Knut","Lund-Andersen, Henrik","Glümer, Charlotte"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-480627"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"cumulative glycemia and microangiopathy in subjects with impaired glucose regulation in the inter000000000099 study","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.diabres.2010.10.017","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-480627","identifier":[{"id":"21130512","type":"pmid"},{"id":"79952042630","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-91eb0361-66a2-4e8b-81f6-7feead3f5952"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-BE7CC8EE6FA24763057B638E3DF9C0A1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.diabres.2010.10.017","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":1,"key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Section of Neurology, Psychiatry and Sensory Sciences"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU"}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Inger Christine","key":"munchpau","last":"Munch","locid":"a1af1ee5-c4b4-44d8-a7af-b7c1ce0882ac","name":"Munch, Inger Christine","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Michael","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"1d460214-c0db-4a15-a3a5-360c73746ed4","name":"Larsen, Michael","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Line","key":"kesselpau","last":"Kessel","locid":"3201472a-2f23-417e-bd01-a7950e2ff370","name":"Kessel, Line","orcid":"0000-0002-9375-1510","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Knut","key":"borchjohnsenpau","last":"Borch-Johnsen","name":"Borch-Johnsen, Knut","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Henrik","key":"lundandersenpau","last":"Lund-Andersen","locid":"5277856f-b771-4658-bb2f-b292ec78ee6f","name":"Lund-Andersen, Henrik","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Charlotte","key":"glümerpau","last":"Glümer","name":"Glümer, Charlotte","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["21130512"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.diabres.2010.10.017","issn":["01688227","15721671"],"issn_org":["01688227"],"issue":"2","journal":"Diabetes Research and Clinical Practice","pages":"226-32","status":"p","title":"Diabetes Research and Clinical Practice","volume":"91"},"review":"No Review","scoID":["2-s2.0-79952042630"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-91eb0361-66a2-4e8b-81f6-7feead3f5952"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Cumulative glycemia and microangiopathy in subjects with impaired glucose regulation in the Inter99 study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-480627","year":"2011","yearsub":"2011"}
{"abstract":"ABSTRACT: BACKGROUND: The human lens is continuously exposed to high levels of light. Ultraviolet radiation is believed to play a causative role in the development of cataract. In vivo, however, the lens is mainly exposed to visible light and the ageing lens absorbs a great part of the short wavelength region of incoming visible light. The aim of the present study was to examine the optical effects on human lenses of short wavelength visible light and ultraviolet radiation. METHODS: Naturally aged human donor lenses were irradiated with UVA (355 nm), violet (400 and 405 nm) and green (532 nm) lasers. The effect of irradiation was evaluated qualitatively by photography and quantitatively by measuring the direct transmission before and after irradiation. Furthermore, the effect of pulsed and continuous laser systems was compared as was the effect of short, intermediate and prolonged exposures. RESULTS: Irradiation with high intensity lasers caused scattering lesions in the human lenses. These effects were more likely to be seen when using pulsed lasers because of the high pulse intensity. Prolonged irradiation with UVA led to photodarkening whereas no detrimental effects were observed after irradiation with visible light. CONCLUSIONS: Irradiation with visible light does not seem to be harmful to the human lens except if the lens is exposed to laser irradiances that are high enough to warrant thermal protein denaturation that is more readily seen using pulsed laser systems.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Kessel, Line","Eskildsen, Lars","Lundeman, Jesper Holm","Jensen, Ole Bjarlin","Larsen, Michael"],"cuid":["S02-099.89-034808"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"optical effects of exposing intact human lenses to ultraviolet radiation and visible light","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/6535976/Optical.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-099.89-034808","identifier":[{"id":"84855180155","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-60f80f17-a41d-4f93-b006-ab8197b206b5","ku-ce90078b-9040-4964-8bfc-3757340f3370"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-F6313B3D29C67646D3C4D9C16766C6D9"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2415-11-41","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/6535976/Optical.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital Herlev and Gentofte"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Photonics Engineering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":3,"key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Section of Neurology, Psychiatry and Sensory Sciences"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU"}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Line","key":"kesselpau","last":"Kessel","name":"Kessel, Line","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Lars","key":"eskildsenpau","last":"Eskildsen","locid":"428a88d1-ca5c-43f0-8b24-f13b76ae767a","name":"Eskildsen, Lars","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper Holm","key":"lundemanpau","last":"Lundeman","name":"Lundeman, Jesper Holm","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ole Bjarlin","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"10955267-cc91-4a3b-8b6c-44dd5c85a87d","name":"Jensen, Ole Bjarlin","orcid":"0000-0002-4295-5951","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Michael","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1186/1471-2415-11-41","issn":["14712415"],"issue":"1","journal":"BMC Ophthalmology","pages":"41","publisher":"BioMed Central Ltd.","status":"p","title":"BMC Ophthalmology","title_org":"B M C Ophthalmology","volume":"11"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84855180155"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-60f80f17-a41d-4f93-b006-ab8197b206b5","ku-ce90078b-9040-4964-8bfc-3757340f3370"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Optical effects of exposing intact human lenses to ultraviolet radiation and visible light","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.89-034808","year":"2011","yearsub":2012}
{"abstract":"OBJECTIVE: Studies on high-risk singleton gestations have shown a preventive effect of progesterone treatment on preterm delivery. This study was conducted to investigate the preventive effect of vaginal micronized progesterone in a large population of twin gestations. METHODS: This was a double-blind, placebo-controlled randomized trial performed in 17 centers in Denmark and Austria. Women with twin gestations were randomized to daily treatment with progesterone pessaries or apparently identical placebo pessaries, starting from 20-24 weeks until 34 weeks' gestation. Primary outcome was incidence of delivery before 34 weeks' gestation. Secondary outcomes were maternal and neonatal complications and long-term infant follow-up, by Ages and Stages Questionnaire (ASQ), 6 months and 18 months after the expected date of delivery. We also updated a published meta-analysis to include our data and those of another recently published twin trial. RESULTS: A total of 677 women were randomized to the two treatments. Two women in the placebo group were lost to follow-up. Baseline characteristics for the groups were similar. Incidence of delivery before 34 weeks was 15.3% in the progesterone group vs 18.5% in the placebo group (odds ratio, 0.8 (95% CI, 0.5-1.2)). Risks of maternal and neonatal complications were comparable for the two groups. Mean ASQ scores at 6 months and 18 months were not significantly different between the two groups (215 for infants in the progesterone group and 218 for infants in the placebo group at 6 months (P = 0.45) and 193 and 194, respectively, at 18 months (P = 0.89)). The meta-analysis gave a pooled odds ratio of 1.06 (95% CI, 0.86-1.31). CONCLUSION: Progesterone treatment did not prevent preterm delivery in twin gestations. There were no harmful effects to fetuses and infants of maternal progesterone treatment.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Rode, Line","Klein, K","Nicolaides, K H","Krampl-Bettelheim, E","Tabor, Ann","Larsen, Helle","Uldbjerg, Niels","Vogel, Ida"],"cuid":["S04-086.54-028569"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corpau":["Ane Lando","Anni Holmskov","Birgit Bødker","Bo Mølholm Hansen","Eva-Christine Weis","Helle Zingenberg","Isolde Strobl","Jan Ramb","Jeanette Tranberg Christensen","Kirsten Riis Andreasen","Lene Sperling","Lillian Skibsted","Lone Krebs","Lone Laursen","PREDICT Group","Stefan Hinterberger"],"country":["Austria","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","King's College London","Medical University of Vienna"],"keyword":["Administration Intravaginal","Adult","Austria","Cervical Length Measurement","Delivery Obstetric","Denmark","Double Blind Method","Female","Humans","Incidence","Journal Article","Logistic Models","Multicenter Study","Pregnancy","Pregnancy Outcome","Premature Birth","Progesterone","Progestins","Randomized Controlled Trial","Research Support Non U S Gov t","Twin study"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"prevention of preterm delivery in twin gestations predict a multicenter randomized placebo controlled trial on the effect of vaginal micronized progesterone","src":["au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/uog.9093","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S04-086.54-028569","identifier":[{"id":"80052295439","type":"scopus"},{"id":"21739497","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-db675806-0e65-4871-b354-d61b5c879788","ku-23bd5f6f-2531-4c56-9e6f-971e707de547","ku-52a7af8a-6455-4e76-9339-ac6b